WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.180
P.S.C.I. 1104, Understanding Political Controversy, with

2
00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:05.280
Professor Trounstein.

3
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.480
This is the full Audito Study Guide.

4
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.280
This recording runs about 90 minutes and covers the entire

5
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.440
semester, 14 concept units,

6
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.980
the seven political debates the course is built around, and a

7
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.480
walk through what the exams actually ask you to do.

8
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.320
A word on how to use it.

9
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.780
There is a shorter 35-minute version of this guide for repeat

10
00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:25.160
listening.

11
00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:26.500
This is the long one.

12
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.680
Use it for a deep pass before the midterm and again before the

13
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:29.980
final.

14
00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.240
You do not have to hold it all at once.

15
00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.980
Where the written guide has a figure, I will describe in words

16
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.080
what the figure shows.

17
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.980
You do not need to be looking at anything while you listen.

18
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.500
Let me start with the shape of the course, because it makes

19
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.540
everything else easier.

20
00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.040
Here is the whole course in one sentence.

21
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.420
Somebody claims that X causes Y.

22
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.380
Should you believe them?

23
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.160
That is the only question this class asks.

24
00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.500
Every reading on the syllabus is a version of it.

25
00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.780
Now, the design of the syllabus.

26
00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.440
Professor Trounstein does not just assign a claim.

27
00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:05.040
She assigns a claim from popular political writing, and then she

28
00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.060
assigns a scholar who tested that claim.

29
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.000
Thomas Frank claims that cultural politics drives working-class

30
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.220
whites to the Republicans.

31
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.640
Larry Bartles checks the survey data.

32
00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:18.140
Michael Bloomberg claims that stop and frisk is not racial

33
00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:18.820
profiling.

34
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.920
Bertrand and Mullenathan randomized names on resumes.

35
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.720
Malcolm Gladwell claims that policing tipped crime downward in

36
00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:26.260
New York.

37
00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:27.960
Stephen Levitt does the arithmetic.

38
00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:29.880
The pairing is the lesson.

39
00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.280
When you read the journalism, ask what evidence would settle it.

40
00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.960
When you read the scholar, name the technique they used.

41
00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.660
That is the habit this course is trying to build, and it is what

42
00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:40.520
the exams test.

43
00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.060
The syllabus says the goal is that you can analyze, explain, and

44
00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.380
apply your knowledge to new and different contexts, which means

45
00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:51.020
expect an unfamiliar claim and a request to evaluate it.

46
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:53.220
One more piece of orientation.

47
00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:59.440
Your grade is 35% final exam, 30% midterm, 30% quizzes across 6

48
00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:03.460
quizzes at 5% each, and 5% attendance through top hat.

49
00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:06.700
Every quiz has a take-home part posted at 9 in the morning the

50
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.540
day before, and an in-class part.

51
00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.500
The take-home part is where you should be building the notes you

52
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:12.420
will want in December.

53
00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.960
Unit 1. Theory-Driven Research

54
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.900
This is Lectures 1 and 2, and it covers Pollock's Introduction

55
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:20.800
and Chapter 1,

56
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.240
Buno de Mosquita and Fowler Chapter 3, and the Fisman, Gelman

57
00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:26.400
and Stephenson piece.

58
00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.760
Science is not a pile of facts.

59
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:29.580
It is a loop.

60
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.140
Picture five boxes in a row.

61
00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:33.600
Box 1. A puzzle.

62
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:34.800
Why does Y vary?

63
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.340
Box 2. A theory.

64
00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:37.740
X causes Y.

65
00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:38.880
Because of some reason.

66
00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:40.620
Box 3. A hypothesis.

67
00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.440
A specific prediction that could turn out wrong.

68
00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:44.960
Box 4. Measurement.

69
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.260
Turning X and Y into actual data.

70
00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.000
Box 5. A test.

71
00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:50.060
Comparing cases.

72
00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:52.860
And then a dashed arrow running from the test back to the

73
00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:53.180
theory.

74
00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.460
Because evidence revises the theory and you go around again.

75
00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.680
That loop is the whole course.

76
00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.720
And notice where the pressure sits.

77
00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.100
It sits on box 3.

78
00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:03.640
Can this prediction lose?

79
00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:04.720
And on box 4.

80
00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:07.120
Did you measure the thing you claim to have measured?

81
00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.560
Almost every criticism in every assigned reading is aimed at one

82
00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:11.540
of those two boxes.

83
00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.100
Now the vocabulary.

84
00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:17.200
A theory is a general explanation of why X causes Y.

85
00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.880
It has to apply beyond the case in front of you.

86
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:20.620
Here is one.

87
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.260
Voting is partly social.

88
00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.220
Because people vote in order to avoid looking like a shirker.

89
00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.700
A hypothesis is one specific, testable prediction that follows

90
00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:30.420
from the theory.

91
00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:33.040
It must name two variables and a direction.

92
00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:35.280
Here is one that follows from that theory.

93
00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.180
Households told that their neighbors will see their turnout

94
00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.340
record will vote at higher rates than households that are not

95
00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:41.700
told.

96
00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:45.600
Falsifiability is the property that makes something scientific.

97
00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.220
If no possible evidence could prove you wrong, you are not doing

98
00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:49.760
science.

99
00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.360
Politics is complicated is not falsifiable.

100
00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:54.160
So it is not a hypothesis.

101
00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.960
And you should be able to separate empirical from normative

102
00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:58.460
claims.

103
00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.780
Empirical claims are about what is.

104
00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.460
And they are settled by evidence.

105
00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.700
Normative claims are about what ought to be.

106
00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:06.700
And they are settled by values.

107
00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.900
Turnout is 60% is empirical.

108
00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:10.960
Turnout should be higher as normative.

109
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.440
Now, the first reading of the semester, which is Fisman, Gelman

110
00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.320
and Stephenson on what they call the statistics that come out of

111
00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:18.640
nowhere.

112
00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.160
This is a short piece and it is very quizzable.

113
00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:23.220
So let me give you their examples.

114
00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:27.260
First, you lose 80% of your body heat through your head.

115
00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.520
That traces back to a 1950s military study in which the subjects

116
00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.500
were wearing Arctic survival suits that covered everything

117
00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:35.340
except the head.

118
00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:37.580
Of course, most of the heat left through the head.

119
00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.400
It was the only thing uncovered.

120
00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:41.280
The Riedel figure is about 10%.

121
00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.360
Second, bribery costs $1 trillion a year.

122
00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.440
That is the midpoint of a range that ran from $600 billion to $1

123
00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.780
.7 trillion built out of a handful of old surveys, taking the

124
00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:55.620
midpoint, deleting the uncertainty.

125
00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:59.020
And the uncertainty was the most honest part of the estimate.

126
00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.360
Third, corruption costs $2.6 trillion.

127
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:07.080
That one traces back to a single sentence, a bullet point, in an

128
00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:09.440
advocacy brief that cited no source at all.

129
00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.620
It was then repeated by the head of the United Nations and by

130
00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.660
the OECD, and after that everybody cited them.

131
00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:19.960
Fourth, Dan Keel's claim that 70% of the world's lawyers are

132
00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:20.340
American.

133
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:24.840
The Riedel share is 25 to 35%, which is roughly the American

134
00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.140
share of world GDP.

135
00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:30.680
From those they draw four rules, and I would memorize all four.

136
00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:31.980
Rule 1.

137
00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:35.240
Trace the number to its original source, not to whoever cited

138
00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:35.480
it.

139
00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:39.180
Studies have shown, and it has been estimated, are red flags

140
00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:41.260
because they are ways of citing nobody.

141
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:42.480
Rule 2.

142
00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.500
Watch for statistics laundering.

143
00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:48.460
A shaky figure gets repeated by a respectable organization, and

144
00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:51.740
then everybody cites the respectable organization instead of the

145
00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:52.480
shaky original.

146
00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:53.760
Rule 3.

147
00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:56.800
A Harvard professor's guess is not a Harvard estimate.

148
00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.400
Institutional prestige attaches itself to numbers that were

149
00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:01.900
never institutional findings.

150
00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:03.220
Rule 4.

151
00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:05.280
Beware the broken telephone effect.

152
00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:08.720
As a number travels, ranges collapse into single points,

153
00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:12.580
kevenots fall off, and narrow findings get generalized far

154
00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:14.080
beyond what the study supported.

155
00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:16.240
Unit 2.

156
00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.740
Concepts and Measurement.

157
00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:18.760
Lecture 4.

158
00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:20.580
Pollock Chapters 2 and 3.

159
00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:23.720
This is the most practically important unit in the course,

160
00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:27.100
because measurement is where most political arguments actually

161
00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:27.760
go wrong.

162
00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.160
You cannot test a claim about the working class until you say

163
00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:32.260
exactly who counts.

164
00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:34.100
Here is the ladder you climb down.

165
00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:36.160
At the top is the concept.

166
00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:38.480
That is the abstract idea you care about.

167
00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:39.220
Democracy.

168
00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:40.240
Ideology.

169
00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:41.420
The working class.

170
00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:42.360
Discrimination.

171
00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:44.920
Concepts are not measurable as they stand.

172
00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:47.260
Next is the conceptual definition.

173
00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:50.440
That is what you mean by the concept, stated in words.

174
00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:51.920
It narrows the idea.

175
00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:55.780
For example, the working class is people whose income depends on

176
00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:57.960
wages rather than on assets or credentials.

177
00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.880
At the bottom is the operational definition.

178
00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:04.720
That is the exact recipe for measuring it, specific enough that

179
00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:07.660
a stranger following your instructions would produce the same

180
00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:08.060
numbers.

181
00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:11.960
For example, the working class is white survey respondents with

182
00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.440
no four-year college degree.

183
00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.980
That bottom step is where arguments are won and lost.

184
00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:18.980
And I will show you that in a moment.

185
00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:20.460
A few more terms.

186
00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:23.840
A variable is the measured thing that varies across cases.

187
00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:26.780
A value is what one particular case scores on it.

188
00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:30.120
And the unit of analysis is what each row of your data actually

189
00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:30.540
is.

190
00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:31.380
A person.

191
00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:32.100
A county.

192
00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:32.700
A country.

193
00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:33.520
A bill.

194
00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:34.240
A resume.

195
00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:37.120
Getting the unit of analysis right matters enormously.

196
00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:39.040
And I will come back to why.

197
00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:42.220
Now the two quality checks you apply to any measure.

198
00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:43.800
Validity asks.

199
00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:46.140
Are you measuring the thing you claim to be measuring?

200
00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:47.580
That is a bias problem.

201
00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:49.560
Reliability asks.

202
00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:52.980
Does the same procedure give you the same answer when you repeat

203
00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:53.240
it?

204
00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:54.880
That is a consistency problem.

205
00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:57.600
Picture four dart boards side by side.

206
00:07:57.980 --> 00:07:58.920
On the first.

207
00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:01.240
Four darts clustered tightly on the bullseye.

208
00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:02.520
Valid and reliable.

209
00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:03.340
On target.

210
00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:03.960
Every time.

211
00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:05.200
On the second.

212
00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:07.360
Four darts clustered just as tightly.

213
00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:09.020
But up in the top right corner.

214
00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:10.460
Well away from the center.

215
00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.500
That is reliable but not valid.

216
00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:13.700
Consistently wrong.

217
00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:14.820
A biased measure.

218
00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:16.140
On the third.

219
00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:19.400
Four darts scattered widely but arranged around the bullseye.

220
00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:21.240
That is valid but not reliable.

221
00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:22.400
Right on average.

222
00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:23.080
But noisy.

223
00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:24.360
On the fourth.

224
00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:26.560
Four darts scattered widely and off center.

225
00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:27.200
Neither.

226
00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:29.900
The dangerous box is the second one.

227
00:08:30.140 --> 00:08:31.140
And here is why.

228
00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:34.420
A measure can be perfectly consistent and perfectly wrong.

229
00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:37.140
Self-reported turnout is the standing example.

230
00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:39.800
People answer the question the same way every time.

231
00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:41.040
So it is reliable.

232
00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:43.200
They also over-report whether they voted.

233
00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.320
So it is invalid.

234
00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.860
And more data never fixes a validity problem.

235
00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:48.240
If your ruler is bent.

236
00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:50.640
Measuring more things with it does not help.

237
00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:51.580
Now.

238
00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:52.740
Levels of measurement.

239
00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:55.280
There are three and you need to be able to name them.

240
00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:59.060
Nominal variables name categories with no inherent order.

241
00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:00.260
Party identification.

242
00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:01.020
Religion.

243
00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:02.220
State of residence.

244
00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:04.440
You can report a mode and percentages.

245
00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.160
Ordinal variables rank categories.

246
00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:09.700
But the gaps between the ranks are not equal.

247
00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:10.560
Strongly agree.

248
00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:11.300
Agree.

249
00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:12.100
Disagree.

250
00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:12.980
Strongly disagree.

251
00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:15.280
Education level is a set of categories.

252
00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:17.440
You can report a mode and a median.

253
00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:21.720
Interval or ratio variables are real numbers with equal gaps.

254
00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:22.780
Turnout percentage.

255
00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:23.840
Income in dollars.

256
00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:24.740
Vote share.

257
00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:25.620
Age in years.

258
00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:27.320
Here you can report a mode.

259
00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:28.100
A median.

260
00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:29.000
A mean.

261
00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:30.480
And a standard divination.

262
00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:32.720
The classic quiz trap on this.

263
00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:35.000
You cannot take a mean of a nominal variable.

264
00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:36.840
The average religion is nonsense.

265
00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:39.800
And ordinal variables get a median rather than a mean.

266
00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:43.180
Because the distance from agree to strongly agree is not a known

267
00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:44.280
quantity you can average.

268
00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:48.660
Now let me show you measurement deciding an entire political

269
00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:49.140
argument.

270
00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:52.480
Because this is lecture five and it will be on a quiz.

271
00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:53.800
Thomas Frank.

272
00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:56.160
In Lie Down for America and in the book.

273
00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:57.020
That followed.

274
00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:00.140
Argues that white working class voters have abandoned the

275
00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:01.060
Democratic Party.

276
00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:04.660
Because cultural politics outweighs economic self-interest.

277
00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:08.200
His evidence includes the observation that the poorest county in

278
00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:11.320
America voted for George Bush by more than 75%.

279
00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:15.040
Larry Bartles responds by asking the measurement question.

280
00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:17.440
Who exactly is the working class?

281
00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:20.760
And when you change the operational definition, the answer

282
00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:21.180
changes.

283
00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:22.940
Here are the numbers.

284
00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:24.580
And these are worth memorizing.

285
00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:27.660
Take the cumulative change in the Democratic share of the two

286
00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.980
-party presidential vote from 1952 through 2004.

287
00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:35.480
That is 14 elections, 52 years, using American National Election

288
00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:36.280
Studies data.

289
00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:39.420
Among all white voters with no college degree.

290
00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:42.280
The Democrats lost 5.9 points.

291
00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:44.140
That looks like support for Frank.

292
00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:46.240
Now control for region.

293
00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:47.040
In the South.

294
00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:50.640
The Democrats lost 19.7 points among that group.

295
00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:51.620
Outside the South.

296
00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:53.720
They lost 1.0 points.

297
00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:55.820
1 point in 52 years.

298
00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:58.600
So essentially nothing happened outside the South.

299
00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:01.980
And what happened in the South was a realignment about race that

300
00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:04.520
began well before the culture war Frank describes.

301
00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:08.740
Now change the operational definition from education to income.

302
00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:11.300
Among white voters in the bottom third of the income

303
00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:11.920
distribution.

304
00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:14.600
The Democrats gained 4.5 points.

305
00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:17.840
The poorest white voters move toward the Democrats, not away

306
00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:18.260
from them.

307
00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:19.700
One concept.

308
00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:21.420
Three operational definitions.

309
00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:22.800
Three different answers.

310
00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:26.460
And Frank never fixed his definition, which is why his thesis

311
00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:27.680
was never really testable.

312
00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:31.360
There is also a validity problem with the education definition

313
00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:32.180
on its face.

314
00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:36.860
In 2004, 40% of white voters without a college degree had family

315
00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:40.580
incomes above $60,000, and more than half of them described

316
00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:42.160
themselves as middle class.

317
00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:45.440
No college degree is simply not a good measure of the working

318
00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:45.880
class.

319
00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:48.240
Two more figures worth having ready.

320
00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:51.620
Bush's margin among the bottom third by income of white non

321
00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:55.640
-college voters in 2004 was under 2 percentage points, which is

322
00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:57.540
not the landslide the argument requires.

323
00:11:57.740 --> 00:12:01.180
And in that same year, when you rank issues by how much weight

324
00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:04.980
non-college whites gave them in their vote, abortion came 13th

325
00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:05.560
out of 15.

326
00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:08.960
Bartels finds economic issues outweighing abortion and vote

327
00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:10.420
choice by more than 2 to 1.

328
00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:14.040
Finally, file away Patrick Ruffini's update in Politico.

329
00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:19.300
In 1996, Bill Clinton won the lowest-income voters by 31 points,

330
00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:22.440
while Bob Dole won the highest-income voters by 16.

331
00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:25.680
There's a 47-point spread across the income scale.

332
00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:29.260
By 2020, that spread had shrunk to roughly 8 points.

333
00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:33.160
Income barely divides the American electorate anymore, which is

334
00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:35.640
itself a striking finding and a good thing to have in your

335
00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:36.040
pocket.

336
00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:39.640
Unit 3. Describing a Variable

337
00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:42.440
Also Lecture 4. Pollock Chapter 3

338
00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:45.440
Before you explain anything, you describe it.

339
00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:47.160
Three questions about any variable.

340
00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:48.140
Where is the middle?

341
00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:49.420
How spread out is it?

342
00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:50.480
And is it lopsided?

343
00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:52.400
Three measures of the middle.

344
00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:55.980
The mode is the most common value, and it is the only average

345
00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:57.620
that works for a nominal variable.

346
00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:00.600
The median is the middle case when you line the cases up in

347
00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:02.520
order, and it ignores extremes.

348
00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:06.280
The mean is the arithmetic average, and it gets dragged toward

349
00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:07.000
outliers.

350
00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:08.920
Two measures of spread.

351
00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:12.600
The range is the highest value minus the lowest, which is crude

352
00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:14.520
and hostage to a single weird case.

353
00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:17.840
The standard dividation is the typical distance of a case from

354
00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:18.300
the mean.

355
00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:21.540
A small standard dividation means the cases are bunched

356
00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:21.880
together.

357
00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:23.140
And skew.

358
00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:24.620
Picture three distributions.

359
00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:26.600
The first is skewed right.

360
00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:29.920
It has a peak on the left and a long tail stretching off to the

361
00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:30.160
right.

362
00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:31.460
The mode is at the peak.

363
00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:33.340
The median is a little to the right of it.

364
00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:36.420
And the mean is further right still, pulled out by the tail.

365
00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:38.100
The second is symmetric.

366
00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:39.260
A classic bell.

367
00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:42.900
The mode, the median and the mean all sit at the same point in

368
00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:43.240
the center.

369
00:13:43.780 --> 00:13:45.260
The third is skewed left.

370
00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:46.680
Long tail to the left.

371
00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:47.660
Peak on the right.

372
00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:49.160
Now the order reverses.

373
00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:50.660
The mean is furthest left.

374
00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:51.760
Then the median.

375
00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:52.580
Then the mode.

376
00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:54.720
The rule that generates all three.

377
00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:56.860
The mean always sits toward the tail.

378
00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:59.340
Income is the standard right skewed example.

379
00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:02.960
A small number of very rich people pull the mean far above the

380
00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:06.680
median, which is exactly why average income flatters a country

381
00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:08.320
and median income describes it.

382
00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:11.520
When an exam asks which measure of central tendency you should

383
00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:13.800
report, the answer is driven by skew.

384
00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:18.520
Now, the ecological fallacy, which costs students points every

385
00:14:18.520 --> 00:14:18.840
year.

386
00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:22.380
The ecological fallacy is inferring something about individuals

387
00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:23.660
from beta about groups.

388
00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:25.380
Poor counties voted for Bush.

389
00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:27.640
Therefore poor people voted for Bush.

390
00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:28.760
That does not follow.

391
00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:31.840
The unit of analysis was the county, not the person.

392
00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:35.100
Within a poor county, it may well be the richer residents who

393
00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:38.420
voted Republican, and in fact that is roughly what the data

394
00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:38.820
show.

395
00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.440
This error appears twice on your syllabus.

396
00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:44.920
It is in Frank's red state map, and it is the fatal flaw in

397
00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:48.160
Messoli's chocolate study, which correlates national chocolate

398
00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:51.680
consumption with national Nobel Prize counts, and never observes

399
00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:53.420
a single laureate eating anything.

400
00:14:54.680 --> 00:14:57.300
Unit 4. Hypothesis and Variables.

401
00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:00.520
Lecture 6. Pollock Chapters 4 and 5.

402
00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:02.440
This is also the day of Quiz 1.

403
00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:06.420
The independent variable, which we call X, is the proposed

404
00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:06.960
cause.

405
00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:09.120
It is the thing that does the explaining.

406
00:15:09.340 --> 00:15:11.880
The dependent variable, Y, is the defect.

407
00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:15.800
It is the thing you are trying to explain, and it depends on X.

408
00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:17.400
That is how you keep them straight.

409
00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:21.080
The null hypothesis is the claim that there is no relationship

410
00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:22.380
between X and Y.

411
00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:25.660
This matters more than it sounds like it should, because the

412
00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:29.300
null hypothesis if what you actually test statistically, you can

413
00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:31.920
only ever reject it, or fail to reject it.

414
00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:35.560
A rival hypothesis is a different explanation for the same

415
00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:38.240
pattern, usually built on a confounding variable.

416
00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:42.080
Two terms that get confused constantly, so get them now.

417
00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:46.360
An antecedent variable comes before X and causes both X and Y.

418
00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:49.700
That is the source of spuriousness, and it destroys a causal

419
00:15:49.700 --> 00:15:50.060
claim.

420
00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:53.960
An intervening variable sits between X and Y and transmits the

421
00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:54.320
defect.

422
00:15:54.520 --> 00:15:57.280
That explains the mechanism, and it leaves the causal claim

423
00:15:57.280 --> 00:15:57.700
standing.

424
00:15:57.920 --> 00:15:58.960
Same-looking diagrams.

425
00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:00.320
Opposite verdicts.

426
00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:02.920
Here is the template for a good hypothesis.

427
00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:07.580
As X increases, Y increases or decreases across some unit of

428
00:16:07.580 --> 00:16:08.080
analysis.

429
00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:09.640
Worked example.

430
00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:12.680
As the perceived chance that neighbors will learn whether you

431
00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:13.460
voted increases,

432
00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:17.300
the probability of voting increases across households.

433
00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:19.060
Notice what that has.

434
00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:20.000
Two variables.

435
00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:20.880
A direction.

436
00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:22.260
A unit of analysis.

437
00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:24.440
And a clear way for it to lose.

438
00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:27.760
Four ways students lose these points on exams.

439
00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:28.800
1.

440
00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:30.020
No veridation.

441
00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:31.360
Why do people vote?

442
00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:33.360
Answered by studying only voters.

443
00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:34.840
There is nothing to compare.

444
00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:35.720
2.

445
00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:37.940
Writing a definition rather than a claim.

446
00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:39.880
Partisans are loyal to their party.

447
00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:41.560
That is what partisan means.

448
00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:43.100
It cannot be false.

449
00:16:43.400 --> 00:16:44.000
3.

450
00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:45.920
Writing about a single case.

451
00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:47.620
Trump won because of the economy.

452
00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:48.480
One case.

453
00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:49.580
No comparison.

454
00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:50.800
Nothing testable.

455
00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:51.680
4.

456
00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:52.420
No direction.

457
00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:55.580
Education is related to turnout does not tell me whether more

458
00:16:55.580 --> 00:16:57.600
education means more voting or less.

459
00:16:58.840 --> 00:16:59.920
Unit 5.

460
00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:02.000
Causality and the Four Hurdles.

461
00:17:02.240 --> 00:17:03.040
Lecture 8.

462
00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:04.940
KELSTEDT and WITTON Chapter 3

463
00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:07.540
And BUENO DE MOSQUITA and FOWLER Chapter 3

464
00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:11.620
If you remember one segment of this recording, make it this one.

465
00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:14.400
This is the single most testable thing in the course.

466
00:17:15.140 --> 00:17:18.020
KELSTEDT and WITTON give you four questions to run any causal

467
00:17:18.020 --> 00:17:18.700
claim through.

468
00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:20.060
Here they are in order.

469
00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:21.280
Hurdle 1.

470
00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:24.860
Is there a credible causal mechanism that connects X to Y?

471
00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:25.960
Not a correlation.

472
00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:29.280
An actual story about how X would produce Y.

473
00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:30.600
Hurdle 2.

474
00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:33.940
Can we eliminate the possibility that Y causes X?

475
00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:35.320
That is reverse causation.

476
00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:36.700
Hurdle 3.

477
00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:39.520
Is there a covariation between X and Y?

478
00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:41.120
Do they in fact move together?

479
00:17:41.620 --> 00:17:42.520
Hurdle 4.

480
00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:46.060
Have we controlled for all the confounding variables Z that

481
00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:49.060
might make the dissociation between X and Y spurious?

482
00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:52.200
You answer each one yes, no, or maybe.

483
00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:54.880
A single confident no defeats the claim.

484
00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:56.940
More yes answers mean more confidence.

485
00:17:57.180 --> 00:17:59.100
And here is the framing that matters.

486
00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:00.820
You never prove causation.

487
00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:02.400
You accumulate confidence.

488
00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:05.180
Now the point KELSTEDT and Wittenhammer.

489
00:18:05.420 --> 00:18:06.020
Hurdle 3.

490
00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:07.220
Covariation.

491
00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:08.700
Is the easy one.

492
00:18:08.920 --> 00:18:12.060
And it is the only hurdle most public arguments ever clear.

493
00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:15.060
Somebody shows you a correlation and treats it as proof.

494
00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:18.200
That is the error the whole course is designed to inoculate you

495
00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:18.560
against.

496
00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:21.220
Picture the hurdles as an actual track.

497
00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:23.120
A causal claim enters at the left.

498
00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:25.900
And it has to jump all four to come out the other side as

499
00:18:25.900 --> 00:18:26.940
something worth believing.

500
00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:29.460
Knock over any one of them and it is done.

501
00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:31.960
Let me define the supporting terms.

502
00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:36.520
A spurious relationship is one where X and Y covery, but only

503
00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:39.180
because some third variable Z causes both of them.

504
00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:41.800
Remove Z and the association vanishes.

505
00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:47.300
A confounding variable is that Z, related to both X and Y, and

506
00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:50.380
if you leave it uncontrolled it produces a misleading picture.

507
00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:54.460
KELSTEDT and Witten run three examples through the hurdles in

508
00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:54.900
the chapter.

509
00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:58.800
Life satisfaction and democratic stability, which is a showcase

510
00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:00.140
for reverse causation,

511
00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:03.560
because you have to ask whether happy citizens produce stable

512
00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:06.720
democracies or stable democracies produce happy citizens.

513
00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:09.960
Race and political participation in the United States.

514
00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:13.040
And whether the Head Start program is effective, where the

515
00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:15.360
problem is that families select into the program,

516
00:19:15.540 --> 00:19:18.580
so the participants and non-participants differ before the

517
00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:19.520
program begins.

518
00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:22.780
Now, why do experiments matter so much here?

519
00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:25.920
Because random assignment clears hurdles two and four

520
00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:26.700
simultaneously.

521
00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:30.460
The researcher sets X, so Y cannot have caused it, and

522
00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:32.520
randomization balances every confounder,

523
00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:34.580
including the ones nobody thought of.

524
00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:37.080
That is a lot of work done by one design choice.

525
00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:39.920
Now, the deeper question.

526
00:19:40.140 --> 00:19:42.380
What does the word cause actually mean?

527
00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:46.160
Puno de Mesquita and Fowler define it counterfactually.

528
00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:49.780
X caused Y for a particular case if Y would have been different

529
00:19:49.780 --> 00:19:50.720
had X been different,

530
00:19:51.040 --> 00:19:52.720
holding everything else fixed.

531
00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:54.140
Cause means made a difference.

532
00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:56.420
It does not mean always followed by.

533
00:19:56.860 --> 00:19:59.560
Formally, each case has two potential outcomes.

534
00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:02.680
The outcome if treated, and the outcome if not treated.

535
00:20:02.680 --> 00:20:05.660
The difference between those two is the treatment effect for

536
00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:06.320
that case.

537
00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:09.400
And the average of that difference across all your cases is

538
00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:10.980
called the average treatment effect,

539
00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:12.880
usually shortened to a TE.

540
00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:15.820
Treatment effects can vary from person to person,

541
00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:19.260
so an average treatment effect can be positive even when the

542
00:20:19.260 --> 00:20:21.460
effect is negative for some individuals.

543
00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:25.020
Now the problem this creates, and it has a formal name,

544
00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:27.480
the fundamental problem of causal inference.

545
00:20:27.480 --> 00:20:30.540
For any single case you only ever observe one of the two

546
00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:31.600
potential outcomes,

547
00:20:31.780 --> 00:20:32.920
you see what happened.

548
00:20:33.140 --> 00:20:34.880
You never see what would have happened.

549
00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:37.520
The counterfactual is permanently unobserved.

550
00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:40.860
The consequence is that a causal effect is never measured

551
00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:41.340
directly.

552
00:20:41.380 --> 00:20:45.400
It is always inferred by finding a believable stand-in for the

553
00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:46.540
missing counterfactual.

554
00:20:46.660 --> 00:20:48.500
That stand-in is your control group.

555
00:20:48.640 --> 00:20:51.960
And now you can see the architecture of this entire course.

556
00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:54.600
Every method you will learn is a different scheme for

557
00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:56.440
constructing a credible control group.

558
00:20:56.440 --> 00:20:59.800
A few conceptual points the chapter raises that are worth

559
00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:00.140
having.

560
00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:02.520
There is no such thing as the cause.

561
00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:04.240
Outcomes have many causes,

562
00:21:04.260 --> 00:21:07.760
and any causal claim is always relative to a specified

563
00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:08.720
counterfactual.

564
00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:12.240
A single counterexample does not refute a causal claim.

565
00:21:12.500 --> 00:21:15.720
Someone who smoked for sixty years and lived to a hundred does

566
00:21:15.720 --> 00:21:17.860
not disprove that smoking causes cancer.

567
00:21:18.140 --> 00:21:21.280
Causation here is probabilistic and about averages, not

568
00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:22.000
deterministic.

569
00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:24.020
Causes precede effects.

570
00:21:24.020 --> 00:21:26.480
Causality does not run backwards in time.

571
00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:29.460
And causation need not imply correlation.

572
00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:31.220
This is the one people forget.

573
00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:35.060
A real causal effect can show zero correlation if it operates in

574
00:21:35.060 --> 00:21:36.940
opposite directions for different groups,

575
00:21:37.120 --> 00:21:40.540
and the effects cancel out, or if people compensate for it

576
00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:41.200
behaviorally.

577
00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:44.840
Now let me run the flagship example, because it is the best

578
00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:46.140
illustration on the syllabus.

579
00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:50.540
In 2012, France Messerly published a paper in the New England

580
00:21:50.540 --> 00:21:53.800
Journal of Medicine correlating chocolate consumption per capita

581
00:21:53.800 --> 00:21:57.440
against Nobel laureates per capita across 23 countries.

582
00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:00.880
Switzerland is at the top on both, at about 12 kilograms of

583
00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:04.480
chocolate per person per year, and about 32 laureates per 10

584
00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:05.280
million people.

585
00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:08.400
Sweden is unusually high on prizes for its chocolate.

586
00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:10.700
China sits at the bottom left on both.

587
00:22:10.920 --> 00:22:15.400
The correlation is R equals 0.79, with a p-value below 0

588
00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:17.060
.0001.

589
00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:20.580
That is strong, statistically significant, and completely real

590
00:22:20.580 --> 00:22:21.400
as a correlation.

591
00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:23.180
Now run the hurdles.

592
00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:24.040
Hurdle 1.

593
00:22:24.280 --> 00:22:24.860
Mechanism.

594
00:22:25.180 --> 00:22:27.960
There is no plausible story by which national chocolate

595
00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:29.840
consumption produces Nobel prizes.

596
00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:30.720
Hurdle 2.

597
00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:31.960
Reverse causation.

598
00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:33.400
Obviously live.

599
00:22:33.560 --> 00:22:37.020
Wealthy, highly educated countries buy more premium chocolate.

600
00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:37.880
Hurdle 3.

601
00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:38.660
Cover edition.

602
00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:41.060
Yes, that is the one thing it has.

603
00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:41.940
Hurdle 4.

604
00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:42.820
Confounders.

605
00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:45.260
Everyone you can name is uncontrolled.

606
00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:46.260
National wealth.

607
00:22:46.480 --> 00:22:47.380
Education systems.

608
00:22:47.680 --> 00:22:48.340
Research funding.

609
00:22:48.620 --> 00:22:50.160
Simply being in Northern Europe.

610
00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:54.480
And on top of that, the unit of analysis is the country, so it

611
00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:56.500
commits the ecological fallacy as well.

612
00:22:56.840 --> 00:22:59.280
Not one laureate's diet was ever observed.

613
00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:01.560
Messerly wrote the paper as a joke.

614
00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:04.400
It is on your syllabus because the joke keeps getting cited

615
00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:05.000
seriously.

616
00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:09.640
Let me give you the four diagrams, because being able to tell

617
00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:11.020
them apart is worth points.

618
00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:12.540
Diagram 1.

619
00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:13.080
X.

620
00:23:13.260 --> 00:23:13.640
An arrow.

621
00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:14.220
Y.

622
00:23:14.380 --> 00:23:15.460
X causes Y.

623
00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:17.220
That is the claim beating made.

624
00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:18.960
Diagram 2.

625
00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:19.680
X.

626
00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:22.480
And an arrow running backwards from Y to X.

627
00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:23.680
Y causes X.

628
00:23:23.960 --> 00:23:24.920
Hurdle 2 fails.

629
00:23:25.820 --> 00:23:26.720
Diagram 3.

630
00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:27.880
Z sits above.

631
00:23:28.100 --> 00:23:30.540
An arrow runs down from Z to X on the left.

632
00:23:30.900 --> 00:23:33.660
And another arrow runs down from Z to Y on the right.

633
00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:37.380
Between X and Y there is only a dashed line, meaning they are

634
00:23:37.380 --> 00:23:39.340
associated but there is no causal arrow.

635
00:23:39.500 --> 00:23:41.020
That is a spurious relationship.

636
00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:42.220
Hurdle 4 fails.

637
00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:44.140
Z is an antecedent variable.

638
00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:45.680
Diagram 4.

639
00:23:45.680 --> 00:23:46.740
X on the left.

640
00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:48.520
An arrow up to M in the middle.

641
00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:50.740
Then an arrow down from M to Y on the right.

642
00:23:50.920 --> 00:23:52.360
X causes Y through M.

643
00:23:52.520 --> 00:23:55.160
That is called mediation, and it is still causal.

644
00:23:55.400 --> 00:23:59.060
M is an intervening variable, and identifying it explains the

645
00:23:59.060 --> 00:24:00.820
relationship rather than undermining it.

646
00:24:01.520 --> 00:24:03.960
Diagrams 3 and 4 look similar on a page.

647
00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:05.440
The verdicts are opposite.

648
00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:07.660
Learn to say which is which and why.

649
00:24:08.820 --> 00:24:09.920
Unit 6.

650
00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:11.680
Research Design and Validity.

651
00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:12.660
Lecture 10.

652
00:24:12.960 --> 00:24:15.500
Johnson, Reynolds and Mykopf Chapter 6.

653
00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:19.280
A research design is the plan that specifies which comparisons

654
00:24:19.280 --> 00:24:20.000
you will make.

655
00:24:20.140 --> 00:24:23.220
The entire art of it is arranging comparisons so that only X

656
00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:24.440
differs between the groups.

657
00:24:24.700 --> 00:24:26.260
Two kinds of validity.

658
00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:29.780
Internal validity is confidence that the relationship you

659
00:24:29.780 --> 00:24:32.300
observed inside your study is genuinely causal.

660
00:24:32.580 --> 00:24:34.940
Experiments are strong on internal validity.

661
00:24:35.440 --> 00:24:38.900
External validity is confidence that the finding generalizes to

662
00:24:38.900 --> 00:24:40.780
other people, places and times.

663
00:24:41.060 --> 00:24:44.180
Laboratory experiments are often weak on external validity.

664
00:24:44.180 --> 00:24:48.380
Now the distinction that appears every single semester, and it

665
00:24:48.380 --> 00:24:51.740
is the single most reliable exam item after the four hurdles.

666
00:24:52.180 --> 00:24:54.640
Random assignment is not random sampling.

667
00:24:55.060 --> 00:24:57.840
Random sampling decides who is in your study.

668
00:24:58.080 --> 00:25:01.900
It draws subjects from a population by chance, and it buys

669
00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:05.300
external validity because your sample resembles the population.

670
00:25:05.800 --> 00:25:08.580
Random assignment decides who receives the treatment.

671
00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:12.440
It sorts subjects into treatment and control by chance, and it

672
00:25:12.440 --> 00:25:13.520
buys internal validity.

673
00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:17.080
Because the two groups are equivalent before you do anything to

674
00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:17.300
them.

675
00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:21.120
A national opinion survey has random sampling and no random

676
00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:21.600
assignment.

677
00:25:21.880 --> 00:25:25.120
A laboratory experiment on 60 undergraduates has random

678
00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:27.080
assignment and no random sampling.

679
00:25:27.320 --> 00:25:29.520
Most studies have exactly one of the two.

680
00:25:29.740 --> 00:25:30.320
No which.

681
00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:32.680
Now the threats to internal validity.

682
00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:35.960
There is a standard list and you should be able to produce it.

683
00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:36.880
History.

684
00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:40.200
Something happens in the outside world between the pretest and

685
00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:42.860
the postist, and gets credited to your treatment.

686
00:25:43.060 --> 00:25:43.620
A scandal.

687
00:25:43.880 --> 00:25:44.360
A recession.

688
00:25:44.620 --> 00:25:45.260
A war.

689
00:25:45.760 --> 00:25:46.360
Maturation.

690
00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:48.900
Subjects change on their own over time.

691
00:25:49.340 --> 00:25:52.720
Teenagers become more political as they age, whether or not you

692
00:25:52.720 --> 00:25:53.600
do anything to them.

693
00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:54.600
Testing.

694
00:25:54.940 --> 00:25:57.460
Taking the protest itself changes the postist.

695
00:25:57.800 --> 00:26:00.660
Asking people about voting makes them more likely to vote.

696
00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:02.100
Instrumentation.

697
00:26:02.100 --> 00:26:04.020
Sometimes called instrument decay.

698
00:26:04.260 --> 00:26:07.320
The measuring device or the human coder drifts over the course

699
00:26:07.320 --> 00:26:07.840
of the study.

700
00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:10.580
A survey question gets reworded halfway through.

701
00:26:11.180 --> 00:26:13.720
Statistical regression or regression to the mean.

702
00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:17.080
Extreme cases drift back toward the average on their own.

703
00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:20.220
If you target the very worst performing schools, they will

704
00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:21.860
improve regardless of what you do,

705
00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:24.500
simply because extreme scores are partly luck.

706
00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:25.940
Selection bias.

707
00:26:26.360 --> 00:26:29.520
The treatment and control groups differed systematically before

708
00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:30.040
the treatment.

709
00:26:30.040 --> 00:26:33.160
This is the single biggest threat in observational work.

710
00:26:33.880 --> 00:26:36.000
Experimental mortality or attrition.

711
00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:40.060
Subjects drop out non-randomly, so the survivors are no longer

712
00:26:40.060 --> 00:26:40.520
comparable.

713
00:26:41.060 --> 00:26:44.280
Demand effects are reactivity, often called the Hawthorne

714
00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:44.600
effect.

715
00:26:44.820 --> 00:26:47.520
Subjects behave differently because they know they are beating

716
00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:47.940
studied.

717
00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:50.440
Diffusion or imitation of the treatment.

718
00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:54.040
The control group gets the treatment anyway, usually because

719
00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:55.480
they talk to the treated group.

720
00:26:55.680 --> 00:26:58.920
Now the elements of an experiment, so you can name them.

721
00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:02.200
There is an experimental or treatment group, a control group,

722
00:27:02.600 --> 00:27:06.400
random assignment, manipulation of the independent variable, and

723
00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:07.880
then a protest and a postist.

724
00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:10.100
There are several design types.

725
00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:13.780
The classical randomized design has a protest and a postist with

726
00:27:13.780 --> 00:27:14.480
both groups.

727
00:27:14.660 --> 00:27:17.620
The postist-only control group design drops the protest

728
00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:19.720
specifically to avoid testing effects.

729
00:27:19.940 --> 00:27:23.560
The Solomon 4 group design combines both so you can isolate the

730
00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:24.320
testing effect.

731
00:27:24.500 --> 00:27:26.960
Factoridal designs cross multiple treatments.

732
00:27:26.960 --> 00:27:30.240
Quasi-experiments include the non-equivalent control group

733
00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:33.820
design and the interrupted time series design, and non

734
00:27:33.820 --> 00:27:36.540
-experimental designs include cross-sectional studies,

735
00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:40.980
longitudinal designs like panel, trend and cohort studies, and

736
00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:42.060
small-n case studies.

737
00:27:42.580 --> 00:27:44.320
Let me give you a rough ranking.

738
00:27:44.660 --> 00:27:46.660
Weakest to strongest on causal inference.

739
00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:49.040
A case study or small-n design.

740
00:27:49.040 --> 00:27:50.800
Nobody assigns X.

741
00:27:51.060 --> 00:27:54.080
Weak on internal validity and weak on external validity.

742
00:27:54.540 --> 00:27:56.160
A cross-sectional survey.

743
00:27:56.420 --> 00:27:57.840
Nobody assigns X.

744
00:27:58.100 --> 00:28:00.660
Weak internally but potentially strong externally if the

745
00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:01.500
sampling is good.

746
00:28:01.720 --> 00:28:03.920
A panel or longitudinal design.

747
00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:08.120
Nobody assigns X, but you can at least see time order, which

748
00:28:08.120 --> 00:28:09.200
helps with hurdle 2.

749
00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:12.300
A quasi-experiment or natural experiment.

750
00:28:12.560 --> 00:28:15.740
Here nature, a policy, or a lottery does the assigning.

751
00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:16.640
Good on both.

752
00:28:16.640 --> 00:28:18.360
A field experiment.

753
00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:21.380
The researcher assigns X in the real world.

754
00:28:21.480 --> 00:28:22.180
Strong on both.

755
00:28:22.600 --> 00:28:24.180
A laboratory experiment.

756
00:28:24.460 --> 00:28:27.460
The researcher assigns X under controlled conditions.

757
00:28:27.800 --> 00:28:30.260
Strongest internally, often weakest externally.

758
00:28:30.660 --> 00:28:32.940
One more concept from this chapter.

759
00:28:33.200 --> 00:28:35.140
Control can be achieved two ways.

760
00:28:35.400 --> 00:28:37.660
By design, through randomization or matching.

761
00:28:37.940 --> 00:28:39.500
Or by statistical adjustment.

762
00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:41.760
Holding Z constant in your analysis.

763
00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:43.140
Those are not equivalent.

764
00:28:43.480 --> 00:28:45.220
And Unit 8 is about Y.

765
00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:47.400
Unit 7.

766
00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:49.840
Experiments and the Logic of Control.

767
00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:51.160
Lecture 12.

768
00:28:51.460 --> 00:28:52.580
Pollock Chapter 6.

769
00:28:52.900 --> 00:28:56.180
Plus Gerber, Green and Lorimer and Bertrand and Mollinathan.

770
00:28:56.400 --> 00:28:57.920
This is also Quiz 3.

771
00:28:57.920 --> 00:29:00.420
Why is randomization so powerful?

772
00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:03.580
One reason, and stated precisely on the exam.

773
00:29:03.940 --> 00:29:07.020
Randomization balances confounders that you never thought of.

774
00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:10.420
Statistical controls can only adjust for variables you measured.

775
00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:13.120
Random assignment balances everything at once.

776
00:29:13.340 --> 00:29:15.760
On average, both observed and unobserved.

777
00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:18.780
That asymmetry is the entire argument for experiments.

778
00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:20.960
The anatomy of an experiment.

779
00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:22.560
Start with a subject pool.

780
00:29:22.980 --> 00:29:25.900
Randomly assign each subject to treatment or control.

781
00:29:26.160 --> 00:29:28.480
Apply the treatment to one group and not the other.

782
00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:30.080
Measure the outcome in both.

783
00:29:30.300 --> 00:29:32.600
The difference between them is the causal effect.

784
00:29:32.800 --> 00:29:35.980
And the check that randomization actually worked is called a

785
00:29:35.980 --> 00:29:36.640
balance table.

786
00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:39.520
You compare the two groups on background characteristics

787
00:29:39.520 --> 00:29:40.960
measured before the treatment.

788
00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:44.100
And if they are statistically indistinguishable, the

789
00:29:44.100 --> 00:29:45.600
randomization did its job.

790
00:29:45.920 --> 00:29:48.040
Gerber, Green and Lorimer report one.

791
00:29:48.260 --> 00:29:49.880
And their groups are indistinguishable.

792
00:29:50.040 --> 00:29:53.120
If a study does not show you a balance table, ask why.

793
00:29:53.620 --> 00:29:56.820
Now the first of the two experiments you will definitely be

794
00:29:56.820 --> 00:29:57.420
tested on.

795
00:29:57.820 --> 00:30:00.360
Gerber, Green and Lorimer, 2008.

796
00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:04.280
In the American Political Science Review, social pressure and

797
00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:08.400
voter turnout, evidence from a large-scale field experiment, 180

798
00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:12.760
,002 households in Michigan, before the August 2006 primary,

799
00:30:13.020 --> 00:30:16.120
households were randomly assigned to a control group or to one

800
00:30:16.120 --> 00:30:16.940
of four mailings.

801
00:30:17.120 --> 00:30:19.520
The control group received no mail.

802
00:30:19.720 --> 00:30:21.800
29.7% of them voted.

803
00:30:22.180 --> 00:30:25.700
The civic duty mailing simply reminded the household that voting

804
00:30:25.700 --> 00:30:26.700
is a civic duty.

805
00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:28.860
31.5% voted.

806
00:30:29.060 --> 00:30:31.540
A lift of 1.8 points over control.

807
00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:34.100
The Hawthorne mailing added one thing.

808
00:30:34.340 --> 00:30:37.400
It told the household that researchers would be studying its

809
00:30:37.400 --> 00:30:37.800
turnout.

810
00:30:38.080 --> 00:30:39.780
32.2% voted.

811
00:30:40.020 --> 00:30:41.760
A lift of 2.5 points.

812
00:30:42.320 --> 00:30:43.880
The self-mailing went further.

813
00:30:44.080 --> 00:30:46.840
It printed the household's own voting record from recent

814
00:30:46.840 --> 00:30:50.340
elections, showing which members had voted and which had not,

815
00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:53.400
and promised to send an updated version after the election.

816
00:30:53.660 --> 00:30:55.560
34.5% voted.

817
00:30:55.560 --> 00:30:57.580
A lift of 4.9 points.

818
00:30:58.160 --> 00:30:59.980
And the neighbor's mailing went furthest.

819
00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:03.320
It printed the household's voting record and the voting records

820
00:31:03.320 --> 00:31:06.320
of its neighbors on the same block, and promised to send

821
00:31:06.320 --> 00:31:08.560
everyone an updated copy after the election.

822
00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:10.780
37.8% voted.

823
00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:12.840
A lift of 8.1 points.

824
00:31:12.840 --> 00:31:14.880
Now, the design question.

825
00:31:15.140 --> 00:31:16.940
Why four treatments instead of one?

826
00:31:17.080 --> 00:31:19.900
Because a single mailer would only tell you that mail works.

827
00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:23.940
This is a dosage design, and dosage designs identify mechanisms.

828
00:31:24.220 --> 00:31:27.860
Each treatment adds exactly one ingredient, and the size of the

829
00:31:27.860 --> 00:31:30.060
jump tells you what that ingredient is worth.

830
00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:32.900
Priming civic duty barely moves anyone.

831
00:31:33.200 --> 00:31:36.180
Adding the knowledge of being observed moves them a bit more.

832
00:31:36.340 --> 00:31:39.500
Showing them their own record moves them substantially more, and

833
00:31:39.500 --> 00:31:42.460
extending that record to the neighbors nearly doubles the effect

834
00:31:42.460 --> 00:31:42.880
again.

835
00:31:42.880 --> 00:31:46.400
The conclusion is that turnout is not driven by information or

836
00:31:46.400 --> 00:31:47.040
by reminders.

837
00:31:47.060 --> 00:31:49.180
It is driven by being seen.

838
00:31:49.180 --> 00:31:53.080
Two pieces of context that make the size of that result clear.

839
00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:56.780
Before the study, a single piece of mail typically move turnout

840
00:31:56.780 --> 00:31:58.660
by less than one percentage point.

841
00:31:58.880 --> 00:32:04.100
8.1 points was unprecedented, and the cost was $1.93 per

842
00:32:04.100 --> 00:32:08.000
additional vote for the neighbor's treatment, and $3.24 for the

843
00:32:08.000 --> 00:32:11.540
self-treatment, compared with roughly $20 per vote for door-to

844
00:32:11.540 --> 00:32:12.200
-door canvassing.

845
00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:15.500
This experiment changed how American campaigns are run.

846
00:32:16.020 --> 00:32:19.980
The obvious objection is ethical, and it is worth being able to

847
00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:23.360
state the treatment worked by shaming people, using public

848
00:32:23.360 --> 00:32:26.680
records, without their consent, and it provoked hundreds of

849
00:32:26.680 --> 00:32:27.760
angry phone calls.

850
00:32:27.940 --> 00:32:30.800
Whether the knowledge gain justifies, that is a live question,

851
00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:33.060
and a good exam answer acknowledges it.

852
00:32:33.580 --> 00:32:34.600
Second experiment.

853
00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:38.900
Bertrand and Molenathan, 2004 in the American Economic Review.

854
00:32:38.900 --> 00:32:42.620
Are Emily and Greg more employable than Lakeisha and Jamal?

855
00:32:42.780 --> 00:32:45.240
A field experiment on labor market discrimination.

856
00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:49.660
They constructed about 5,000 resumes and sent them to more than

857
00:32:49.660 --> 00:32:54.200
1,300 job advertisements in Boston and Chicago, in sales,

858
00:32:54.500 --> 00:32:57.620
administrative support, clerical and customer service

859
00:32:57.620 --> 00:32:58.140
categories.

860
00:32:58.400 --> 00:33:02.320
Over roughly a year starting in July 2001, the resumes were

861
00:33:02.320 --> 00:33:05.700
identical in every respect except the name at the top, which was

862
00:33:05.700 --> 00:33:08.620
randomly assigned to sound either white or African American.

863
00:33:08.900 --> 00:33:11.580
Based on Massachusetts birth certificate data.

864
00:33:11.880 --> 00:33:12.740
The result.

865
00:33:13.240 --> 00:33:17.540
Resumes with white-sounding names received a callback 9.65% of

866
00:33:17.540 --> 00:33:17.940
the time.

867
00:33:18.140 --> 00:33:21.580
Resumes with African American-sounding names received a callback

868
00:33:21.580 --> 00:33:23.580
6.45% of the time.

869
00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:27.660
The gap is 3.2 percentage points, which means white names

870
00:33:27.660 --> 00:33:29.660
received 50% more callbacks.

871
00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:32.840
The authors calculate that the gap is equivalent to about eight

872
00:33:32.840 --> 00:33:34.580
additional years of work experience.

873
00:33:34.580 --> 00:33:38.280
Now, the design point, which is the reason this reading is

874
00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:39.800
paired with Bloomberg's op-ed.

875
00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:43.640
In any observational study of hiring, employers observe far more

876
00:33:43.640 --> 00:33:47.260
about applicants than researchers do, so any observed gap might

877
00:33:47.260 --> 00:33:48.640
reflect something unmeasured.

878
00:33:48.820 --> 00:33:51.900
Randomly assigning the name to an otherwise identical resume

879
00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:53.820
eliminates that possibility entirely.

880
00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:56.800
There is nothing left that could differ except the name.

881
00:33:56.800 --> 00:34:00.460
And there is a second finding that is counterintuitive and very

882
00:34:00.460 --> 00:34:00.940
quizzable.

883
00:34:01.140 --> 00:34:04.260
They also varied resume quality, adding experience,

884
00:34:04.620 --> 00:34:06.120
certifications and skills.

885
00:34:06.340 --> 00:34:10.540
For white names, improving the resume-raised callbacks by 2.29

886
00:34:10.540 --> 00:34:14.800
percentage points, from 8.50% to 10.79%.

887
00:34:14.800 --> 00:34:18.480
For African American names, improving the resume-raised

888
00:34:18.480 --> 00:34:24.460
callbacks by only 0.51 points, from 6.19% to 6.70%,

889
00:34:24.460 --> 00:34:27.460
and that difference was not statistically significant.

890
00:34:27.720 --> 00:34:30.900
So the racial gap widens as qualifications improve.

891
00:34:31.360 --> 00:34:34.560
Investing in your credentials pays off substantially less if

892
00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:36.120
your name sounds African American.

893
00:34:36.120 --> 00:34:39.500
That is a much harder finding than a simple average gap, and it

894
00:34:39.500 --> 00:34:40.360
is the one to cite.

895
00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:45.100
Unit 8. Controlling for Confounding Factors Without an

896
00:34:45.100 --> 00:34:45.520
Experiment.

897
00:34:45.820 --> 00:34:49.980
Lectures 13 and 14. Pollock Chapter 7 and Lely and Nagler

898
00:34:49.980 --> 00:34:50.620
Chapter 2.

899
00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:53.540
Most political questions cannot be randomized.

900
00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:57.880
You cannot assign somebody a race, a region, an income, or a

901
00:34:57.880 --> 00:34:58.180
childhood.

902
00:34:58.520 --> 00:34:59.700
So what do you do?

903
00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:03.740
You use controlled comparison, split the sample by the suspected

904
00:35:03.740 --> 00:35:04.540
confounder Z,

905
00:35:04.780 --> 00:35:08.080
and then look at the relationship between X and Y inside each

906
00:35:08.080 --> 00:35:08.820
slice separately.

907
00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:12.360
In practice this is a cross-tabulation, or adding control

908
00:35:12.360 --> 00:35:13.600
variables to a regression.

909
00:35:14.080 --> 00:35:17.040
Three things can happen, and you should be able to name all

910
00:35:17.040 --> 00:35:17.280
three.

911
00:35:17.740 --> 00:35:20.760
1. The relationship holds inside every slice.

912
00:35:20.960 --> 00:35:22.320
Z was not doing the work.

913
00:35:22.480 --> 00:35:23.140
X survives.

914
00:35:23.720 --> 00:35:26.600
2. The relationship disappears in every slice.

915
00:35:26.860 --> 00:35:27.980
Z was causing both.

916
00:35:28.200 --> 00:35:30.200
The original relationship was spurious.

917
00:35:30.860 --> 00:35:34.600
3. The relationship is strong in one slice and absent in

918
00:35:34.600 --> 00:35:34.880
another.

919
00:35:34.880 --> 00:35:37.880
That is an interaction, sometimes called specification.

920
00:35:38.240 --> 00:35:40.820
X matters, but only under certain conditions.

921
00:35:41.640 --> 00:35:44.400
Bartle's region analysis is exactly this procedure.

922
00:35:44.680 --> 00:35:45.280
Uncontrolled.

923
00:35:45.600 --> 00:35:49.340
White non-college voters shifted 5.9 points Republican.

924
00:35:49.660 --> 00:35:50.620
Control for region.

925
00:35:50.980 --> 00:35:55.120
And it is 19.7 points in the South against 1.0 outside it.

926
00:35:55.260 --> 00:35:57.540
The confounder was doing almost all the work.

927
00:35:57.540 --> 00:36:01.360
Now here is the permanent limitation, and it is the standard

928
00:36:01.360 --> 00:36:04.360
critique to deploy on any exam question about a survey-based

929
00:36:04.360 --> 00:36:05.140
causal claim.

930
00:36:05.320 --> 00:36:08.440
You can only control for variables you measured and thought of.

931
00:36:08.720 --> 00:36:11.000
Randomization balances the ones you did not.

932
00:36:11.200 --> 00:36:14.680
That asymmetry is why a well-designed experiment on a small

933
00:36:14.680 --> 00:36:17.900
artificial question often beats an enormous observational study

934
00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:18.680
on the real one.

935
00:36:18.680 --> 00:36:22.940
Now, Lili and Nagler, this chapter is on the syllabus for a

936
00:36:22.940 --> 00:36:23.440
subtle reason.

937
00:36:23.720 --> 00:36:26.240
It shows you when to control and when not to.

938
00:36:26.540 --> 00:36:29.880
They pose two questions that sound identical and are not.

939
00:36:30.400 --> 00:36:31.400
First question.

940
00:36:31.600 --> 00:36:33.860
Are poor people underrepresented among voters?

941
00:36:34.160 --> 00:36:36.260
For this you should not control for anything.

942
00:36:36.500 --> 00:36:37.680
Just compare shares.

943
00:36:37.940 --> 00:36:41.000
Controlling for age or education here would answer a different

944
00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:41.900
question entirely.

945
00:36:41.900 --> 00:36:43.480
Second question.

946
00:36:43.720 --> 00:36:47.100
Is a poor person less likely to vote than an otherwise identical

947
00:36:47.100 --> 00:36:47.860
richer person?

948
00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:51.760
For this you must control, because income rises with age and

949
00:36:51.760 --> 00:36:52.880
young people vote less.

950
00:36:53.200 --> 00:36:56.240
So an uncontrolled income gap partly reflects age.

951
00:36:56.600 --> 00:36:59.120
Their tool for the first question is called the

952
00:36:59.120 --> 00:37:00.340
representativeness ratio.

953
00:37:00.700 --> 00:37:04.440
Take a group's share of actual voters and divide by that group's

954
00:37:04.440 --> 00:37:06.700
share of the citizen voting age population.

955
00:37:06.920 --> 00:37:10.720
A ratio of 1.0 means the group is proportionally represented.

956
00:37:10.720 --> 00:37:12.620
Above 1 means overrepresented.

957
00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:14.720
Below 1 means underrepresented.

958
00:37:15.280 --> 00:37:18.800
Here are the results, and the headline is that almost nothing

959
00:37:18.800 --> 00:37:19.240
moved.

960
00:37:19.440 --> 00:37:22.580
The bottom fifth of the income distribution had a

961
00:37:22.580 --> 00:37:26.260
representativeness ratio of 0.79 in 1972.

962
00:37:26.640 --> 00:37:29.220
In 2008 it was 0.79.

963
00:37:29.540 --> 00:37:30.260
Identical.

964
00:37:30.440 --> 00:37:34.500
36 years of dramatically rising economic inequality, and the

965
00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:37.020
income bias in turnout did not increase at all.

966
00:37:37.020 --> 00:37:42.580
The top fifth went from 1.23 to 1.17, which is actually a slight

967
00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:44.140
decline in overrepresentation.

968
00:37:44.340 --> 00:37:47.420
The middle fifth sat at about 1.00 throughout.

969
00:37:47.740 --> 00:37:49.980
Where things did change was race.

970
00:37:50.200 --> 00:37:55.880
Black turnout rose from 52.9% in 1972 to 64.7% in

971
00:37:55.880 --> 00:37:56.560
2008,

972
00:37:56.560 --> 00:38:01.240
and the representativeness ratio went from 0.82 to 1.02.

973
00:38:01.240 --> 00:38:03.900
From clearly underrepresented to rough parity.

974
00:38:04.180 --> 00:38:09.440
Hispanic voters moved much less, from 0.69 in 1976 to 0

975
00:38:09.440 --> 00:38:11.240
.78 in 2008.

976
00:38:11.480 --> 00:38:15.020
Women went from 0.99 to 1.03.

977
00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:18.580
Women were slightly less likely to vote than men in 1972,

978
00:38:18.940 --> 00:38:22.820
and since 1984 they have been consistently more likely, by more

979
00:38:22.820 --> 00:38:24.600
than four points by 2008.

980
00:38:24.600 --> 00:38:26.260
And age matters.

981
00:38:26.560 --> 00:38:30.080
Voters aged 18 to 24 are the lowest turnout group in every

982
00:38:30.080 --> 00:38:33.100
single year, with a ratio around 0.77.

983
00:38:33.440 --> 00:38:38.400
Meanwhile the 76 to 84 group went from underrepresented at 0.91

984
00:38:38.400 --> 00:38:40.360
to overrepresented at 1.11.

985
00:38:40.940 --> 00:38:44.000
Now three methodological points from this chapter that are very

986
00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:44.800
exam-friendly.

987
00:38:45.340 --> 00:38:47.180
First, the denominator problem.

988
00:38:47.340 --> 00:38:50.420
Turnout has not declined, measured against citizens of voting

989
00:38:50.420 --> 00:38:50.760
age.

990
00:38:50.760 --> 00:38:56.180
It ranged from a low of 58.4% in 1996 to a high of 65

991
00:38:56.180 --> 00:38:59.780
.5% in 1992, with no downward trend.

992
00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:03.460
The famous decline everybody cites is an artifact of using the

993
00:39:03.460 --> 00:39:06.200
total voting age population as the denominator

994
00:39:06.200 --> 00:39:11.180
while the non-citizen share grew from under 2% in 1972 to 8.4%

995
00:39:11.180 --> 00:39:12.160
in 2008.

996
00:39:12.380 --> 00:39:15.620
Same data, different denominator, opposite conclusion.

997
00:39:16.240 --> 00:39:19.640
Second, relative measures beat absolute ones over time.

998
00:39:19.640 --> 00:39:22.620
They use income quintiles rather than dollar brackets, and

999
00:39:22.620 --> 00:39:24.980
education thirds rather than degree categories,

1000
00:39:25.220 --> 00:39:27.380
because the meaning of a category changes.

1001
00:39:27.620 --> 00:39:32.960
College graduates went from 12% of adults in 1972 to 29.4% in

1002
00:39:32.960 --> 00:39:33.700
2008,

1003
00:39:33.940 --> 00:39:38.420
while people without a high school diploma fell from 41.8% to 13

1004
00:39:38.420 --> 00:39:38.720
.4%.

1005
00:39:39.440 --> 00:39:43.180
Measured in absolute categories, the least educated group looks

1006
00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:44.740
like it collapsed in representation,

1007
00:39:44.740 --> 00:39:49.660
from 0.79 to 0.62, measured as the bottom third.

1008
00:39:49.980 --> 00:39:51.200
It did not move at all.

1009
00:39:51.380 --> 00:39:54.720
Same data, two opposite stories, purely from the choice of

1010
00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:55.060
measure.

1011
00:39:55.680 --> 00:39:57.120
Third, measurement error.

1012
00:39:57.540 --> 00:40:01.200
Self-reported turnout produces over-reporting, and higher-status

1013
00:40:01.200 --> 00:40:02.360
people over-report more,

1014
00:40:02.560 --> 00:40:05.040
so the error is systematic rather than random.

1015
00:40:05.300 --> 00:40:07.980
Their assumption is that the differences in misreporting across

1016
00:40:07.980 --> 00:40:09.700
groups are stable over time,

1017
00:40:09.900 --> 00:40:12.660
which lets them study change even if the levels are off.

1018
00:40:12.660 --> 00:40:15.500
And one honest caveat they raise themselves,

1019
00:40:15.840 --> 00:40:18.940
measured against the total voting age population rather than

1020
00:40:18.940 --> 00:40:19.380
citizens,

1021
00:40:19.700 --> 00:40:21.160
income bias has increased.

1022
00:40:21.380 --> 00:40:25.220
2008 bottom quintile is 0.79 on citizens,

1023
00:40:25.520 --> 00:40:28.860
but 0.75 on the full voting age population.

1024
00:40:29.180 --> 00:40:29.980
As they put it,

1025
00:40:30.180 --> 00:40:33.200
the question is whether you care about representation of the

1026
00:40:33.200 --> 00:40:33.600
eligible,

1027
00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:34.880
or of the available.

1028
00:40:35.960 --> 00:40:39.100
Unit 9, Survey Research, Drawing a Sample,

1029
00:40:39.100 --> 00:40:41.320
Lecture 18, Pollock Chapter 8,

1030
00:40:41.460 --> 00:40:42.760
and more and nots.

1031
00:40:43.080 --> 00:40:45.880
The population is everyone you want information about.

1032
00:40:46.060 --> 00:40:48.400
The sample is the part you actually examine.

1033
00:40:48.620 --> 00:40:50.040
A census measures everyone.

1034
00:40:50.340 --> 00:40:52.240
A sample survey measures a part.

1035
00:40:52.500 --> 00:40:55.780
The sampling frame is the list you actually draw from.

1036
00:40:55.960 --> 00:40:58.980
The gap between the frame and the population is where coverage

1037
00:40:58.980 --> 00:40:59.640
error lives,

1038
00:40:59.640 --> 00:41:02.380
and it is a bigger deal than most people realize.

1039
00:41:02.960 --> 00:41:05.240
Now two terms you must not mix up.

1040
00:41:05.400 --> 00:41:07.840
A parameter is a number describing the population.

1041
00:41:07.840 --> 00:41:10.000
It is fixed and usually unknown.

1042
00:41:10.280 --> 00:41:12.800
A statistic is a number computed from your sample.

1043
00:41:13.000 --> 00:41:13.620
It is known,

1044
00:41:13.780 --> 00:41:15.860
and it varies from sample to sample.

1045
00:41:16.180 --> 00:41:18.040
Statistics estimate parameters.

1046
00:41:18.340 --> 00:41:21.180
Two bad designs to be able to name and criticize.

1047
00:41:21.780 --> 00:41:25.000
A convenient sample takes whoever is easiest to reach,

1048
00:41:25.180 --> 00:41:27.640
standing in a mall and interviewing passers-by,

1049
00:41:27.880 --> 00:41:29.560
systematically unrepresentative.

1050
00:41:29.560 --> 00:41:33.780
A voluntary response sample lets people select themselves in.

1051
00:41:33.920 --> 00:41:37.180
Call in polls, write in surveys, opt in web panels.

1052
00:41:37.520 --> 00:41:39.820
These over-represent people with strong opinions,

1053
00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:42.180
and usually strong negative opinions,

1054
00:41:42.460 --> 00:41:44.700
because irritation motivates participation.

1055
00:41:45.080 --> 00:41:46.460
Now the good designs.

1056
00:41:46.740 --> 00:41:49.300
A simple random sample of size N is chosen

1057
00:41:49.300 --> 00:41:51.880
so that every set of N individuals in the population

1058
00:41:51.880 --> 00:41:55.000
has an equal chance of being selected as the sample.

1059
00:41:55.240 --> 00:41:56.620
Note that wording precisely,

1060
00:41:56.880 --> 00:41:58.640
because it is a classic exam trap,

1061
00:41:58.640 --> 00:42:01.740
saying every individual has an equal chance is necessary,

1062
00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:02.940
but not sufficient.

1063
00:42:03.200 --> 00:42:06.260
A probability sample more broadly is any design,

1064
00:42:06.440 --> 00:42:07.780
where each unit has a known,

1065
00:42:07.980 --> 00:42:09.840
non-zero chance of selection.

1066
00:42:10.080 --> 00:42:13.440
A stratified random sample divides the population into strata

1067
00:42:13.440 --> 00:42:15.760
and draws a simple random sample within each,

1068
00:42:16.000 --> 00:42:18.660
which guarantees representation of small groups.

1069
00:42:18.860 --> 00:42:22.700
A multi-stage sample uses successive stages of random selection,

1070
00:42:22.940 --> 00:42:25.660
and that is what national pollsters actually do.

1071
00:42:25.660 --> 00:42:28.100
Now, sampling variability.

1072
00:42:28.480 --> 00:42:31.000
Different random samples give different statistics.

1073
00:42:31.340 --> 00:42:33.320
If you repeated the sampling many times,

1074
00:42:33.500 --> 00:42:35.440
the statistics would form a distribution,

1075
00:42:35.440 --> 00:42:37.580
and when the design is unbiased,

1076
00:42:37.740 --> 00:42:39.920
that distribution is centered on the true parameter.

1077
00:42:40.260 --> 00:42:43.920
Which brings us to the most important conceptual distinction in

1078
00:42:43.920 --> 00:42:44.380
this unit.

1079
00:42:44.380 --> 00:42:46.140
Bias versus variability.

1080
00:42:46.660 --> 00:42:47.980
Picture the dartboards again.

1081
00:42:48.300 --> 00:42:50.500
Bias is shots consistently off-center.

1082
00:42:51.080 --> 00:42:53.160
Variability is shots widely scattered.

1083
00:42:53.540 --> 00:42:55.780
To reduce bias you need a better design,

1084
00:42:56.020 --> 00:42:57.540
specifically random sampling.

1085
00:42:57.820 --> 00:43:00.420
To reduce variability you need a larger sample.

1086
00:43:00.680 --> 00:43:02.780
Those are different problems with different fixes,

1087
00:43:02.780 --> 00:43:06.400
and confusing them is the root of most bad polling commentary.

1088
00:43:06.620 --> 00:43:07.880
The margin of error.

1089
00:43:07.880 --> 00:43:11.840
The quick rule for a 95% confidence statement about a proportion

1090
00:43:11.840 --> 00:43:13.040
is that the margin of error

1091
00:43:13.040 --> 00:43:16.100
is approximately 1 divided by the square root of n.

1092
00:43:16.400 --> 00:43:20.840
So a sample of 1,000 gives you about plus or minus 3.2%.

1093
00:43:20.840 --> 00:43:24.020
A sample of 400 gives you about plus or minus 5%.

1094
00:43:24.420 --> 00:43:28.060
A sample of 2,000 gives you about plus or minus 2.2%.

1095
00:43:28.720 --> 00:43:31.720
Picture that as a curve dropping steeply at first and then

1096
00:43:31.720 --> 00:43:32.440
flattening out.

1097
00:43:32.560 --> 00:43:35.280
To have your margin of error you need 4 times the sample.

1098
00:43:35.280 --> 00:43:39.000
That is why national polls cluster between 800 and 1,500

1099
00:43:39.000 --> 00:43:39.860
respondents.

1100
00:43:40.220 --> 00:43:43.000
Pass that you are paying a great deal for very little precision.

1101
00:43:43.300 --> 00:43:45.440
Two things that trip students up.

1102
00:43:45.560 --> 00:43:48.100
First, a confidence statement has two parts,

1103
00:43:48.260 --> 00:43:51.100
a margin of error and a confidence level and both have to be

1104
00:43:51.100 --> 00:43:51.480
reported.

1105
00:43:51.840 --> 00:43:54.660
Second, the size of the population does not matter,

1106
00:43:54.780 --> 00:43:58.360
provided the population is at least about 100 times the size of

1107
00:43:58.360 --> 00:43:58.800
the sample.

1108
00:43:59.020 --> 00:44:02.900
A sample of 1,500 is exactly as informative for Nashville as it

1109
00:44:02.900 --> 00:44:04.600
is for the entire United States.

1110
00:44:04.600 --> 00:44:07.660
That is deeply counterintuitive and it is a favorite exam

1111
00:44:07.660 --> 00:44:08.160
question.

1112
00:44:08.780 --> 00:44:12.740
The historical example to have ready is the Literary Digest poll

1113
00:44:12.740 --> 00:44:13.840
of 1936.

1114
00:44:14.140 --> 00:44:17.820
The magazine mailed out 10 million ballots and received 2.4

1115
00:44:17.820 --> 00:44:18.500
million back

1116
00:44:18.500 --> 00:44:21.680
and predicted that Alf Landon would defeat Franklin Roosevelt.

1117
00:44:21.920 --> 00:44:25.760
Roosevelt won in a landslide, taking 46 of 48 states.

1118
00:44:26.220 --> 00:44:27.300
What went wrong?

1119
00:44:27.480 --> 00:44:30.260
Their sampling frame came from automobile registrations,

1120
00:44:30.260 --> 00:44:33.440
telephone directories and magazine subscription lists,

1121
00:44:33.760 --> 00:44:37.580
all of which meant affluent in 1936, in the middle of the

1122
00:44:37.580 --> 00:44:37.980
Depression.

1123
00:44:38.160 --> 00:44:39.280
That is coverage error.

1124
00:44:39.460 --> 00:44:42.920
And on top of that, respondents chose whether to mail the ballot

1125
00:44:42.920 --> 00:44:43.280
back,

1126
00:44:43.520 --> 00:44:44.920
which is voluntary response.

1127
00:44:45.580 --> 00:44:48.660
Meanwhile, George Gallop used a far smaller but properly

1128
00:44:48.660 --> 00:44:50.360
designed sample and guide right.

1129
00:44:50.600 --> 00:44:51.540
The lesson is stark.

1130
00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:55.060
2.4 million responses could not save a biased design,

1131
00:44:55.060 --> 00:44:57.800
and a few thousand well-chosen ones did the job.

1132
00:44:57.980 --> 00:45:00.160
A huge sample does not fix bias.

1133
00:45:00.360 --> 00:45:02.940
It just gives you a very precise wrong answer.

1134
00:45:04.200 --> 00:45:05.140
Unit 10.

1135
00:45:05.340 --> 00:45:07.160
Survey Research and Polling Errors.

1136
00:45:07.340 --> 00:45:08.320
Lecture 19.

1137
00:45:08.640 --> 00:45:09.680
Moore and Notts.

1138
00:45:09.820 --> 00:45:13.140
Plus Nate Cohn, Joshua Clinton and Sunshine Hillegus.

1139
00:45:13.300 --> 00:45:14.680
This is also Quiz 4.

1140
00:45:15.160 --> 00:45:17.100
This unit has one central idea,

1141
00:45:17.300 --> 00:45:19.480
and if you take nothing else, take this.

1142
00:45:19.480 --> 00:45:22.560
The reported margin of error is the smallest error in a poll.

1143
00:45:23.100 --> 00:45:26.200
The margin of error covers random sampling error and nothing

1144
00:45:26.200 --> 00:45:26.620
else.

1145
00:45:26.820 --> 00:45:28.460
Here is what it does not cover.

1146
00:45:28.860 --> 00:45:31.080
Coverage Error, or Under Coverage,

1147
00:45:31.260 --> 00:45:33.680
Groups missing from the sampling frame entirely.

1148
00:45:33.960 --> 00:45:37.180
Landline-only frames missing cell phone-only households.

1149
00:45:37.660 --> 00:45:38.700
Non-English speakers.

1150
00:45:39.140 --> 00:45:40.600
People without stable addresses.

1151
00:45:41.400 --> 00:45:42.280
Non-response.

1152
00:45:42.540 --> 00:45:45.180
Selected people who cannot be reached or who refuse.

1153
00:45:45.540 --> 00:45:48.160
This is the dominant practical problem in modern polling,

1154
00:45:48.160 --> 00:45:50.340
because response rates have collapsed.

1155
00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:53.180
Response Bias or Measurement Error.

1156
00:45:53.300 --> 00:45:55.520
The respondent gives an inaccurate answer.

1157
00:45:55.780 --> 00:45:56.180
Lying.

1158
00:45:56.480 --> 00:45:57.800
Social Desirability.

1159
00:45:58.220 --> 00:46:00.540
Memory Error or Interviewer Effects,

1160
00:46:00.780 --> 00:46:02.880
including Race of Interviewer Effects.

1161
00:46:03.420 --> 00:46:05.240
Question Wording and Question Order.

1162
00:46:05.600 --> 00:46:07.680
Leading or Loaded Wording changes answers,

1163
00:46:07.860 --> 00:46:10.680
and so does what you asked immediately before.

1164
00:46:11.180 --> 00:46:12.260
Adjustment Error.

1165
00:46:12.460 --> 00:46:12.980
That is waiting.

1166
00:46:13.400 --> 00:46:16.360
The pollsters' decisions about what the population looks like.

1167
00:46:16.360 --> 00:46:18.180
And likely voter modeling.

1168
00:46:18.480 --> 00:46:20.400
Deciding who will actually turn out.

1169
00:46:20.520 --> 00:46:23.980
Hillegas argues this is frequently the most consequential choice

1170
00:46:23.980 --> 00:46:24.900
a pollster makes.

1171
00:46:25.140 --> 00:46:26.840
Her example is Gallup in 2000,

1172
00:46:27.160 --> 00:46:30.540
where changes to the likely voter screen produced a 19-point

1173
00:46:30.540 --> 00:46:33.220
swing in the reported margin over a matter of days,

1174
00:46:33.400 --> 00:46:36.500
which was an artifact of the model rather than real movement in

1175
00:46:36.500 --> 00:46:37.140
the electorate.

1176
00:46:37.140 --> 00:46:39.420
All of those are non-sampling errors,

1177
00:46:39.680 --> 00:46:40.680
they produce bias,

1178
00:46:40.920 --> 00:46:43.760
and a bigger sample does not fix a single one of them.

1179
00:46:44.320 --> 00:46:46.760
Now, the best demonstration on the syllabus.

1180
00:46:46.960 --> 00:46:50.360
Nate Cohn, writing for the New York Times Upshot in 2016,

1181
00:46:50.840 --> 00:46:53.040
ran what amounts to a natural experiment.

1182
00:46:53.040 --> 00:46:56.680
He took one poll of 867 likely Florida voters,

1183
00:46:56.900 --> 00:47:00.200
and handed the identical raw interview data to four outside

1184
00:47:00.200 --> 00:47:00.920
polling teams.

1185
00:47:01.100 --> 00:47:01.920
Same respondents.

1186
00:47:02.240 --> 00:47:02.960
Same answers.

1187
00:47:03.280 --> 00:47:03.980
Same everything.

1188
00:47:03.980 --> 00:47:07.120
Penscone Berlin reported Clinton plus 4.

1189
00:47:07.320 --> 00:47:10.440
Charles Franklin of Marquette reported Clinton plus 3.

1190
00:47:10.800 --> 00:47:13.180
Echelon Insights reported Clinton plus 1.

1191
00:47:13.400 --> 00:47:16.980
The Time Zone Upshot and Siena team reported Clinton plus 1.

1192
00:47:17.240 --> 00:47:20.920
And a team from Stanford, Columbia and Microsoft reported Trump

1193
00:47:20.920 --> 00:47:21.500
plus 1.

1194
00:47:21.680 --> 00:47:25.280
That is a five-point spread produced entirely by two judgment

1195
00:47:25.280 --> 00:47:25.860
calls.

1196
00:47:26.000 --> 00:47:28.940
How to weight the sample, and who counts as a likely voter?

1197
00:47:28.940 --> 00:47:31.380
Their implied electorate stiffered as well.

1198
00:47:31.600 --> 00:47:34.720
The white share ranged from about 65 to about 70 percent,

1199
00:47:34.960 --> 00:47:38.360
and the partisan composition ranged from Democrats plus 5 to

1200
00:47:38.360 --> 00:47:39.700
Republicans plus 1.

1201
00:47:40.300 --> 00:47:42.300
Cohn's conclusion is the line to quote,

1202
00:47:42.460 --> 00:47:44.040
The margin of error, he wrote,

1203
00:47:44.280 --> 00:47:47.240
does not even come close to capturing total survey error.

1204
00:47:47.720 --> 00:47:50.580
Joshua Clinton, who was on the Vanderbilt faculty,

1205
00:47:51.040 --> 00:47:53.080
made the same point differently in 2024.

1206
00:47:53.520 --> 00:47:56.980
He took a single survey and applied 16 defensible weighting

1207
00:47:56.980 --> 00:47:57.800
schemes to it.

1208
00:47:57.800 --> 00:48:02.420
The estimates ranged from Harris plus 3.8 to Harris plus 9.4,

1209
00:48:02.660 --> 00:48:05.220
nearly six points of varination from weighting alone,

1210
00:48:05.500 --> 00:48:08.200
with no change whatsoever in the underlying data.

1211
00:48:08.480 --> 00:48:12.000
And here is the number that explains why weighting carries so

1212
00:48:12.000 --> 00:48:12.760
much of the load.

1213
00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:16.400
Only about five out of every hundred contacted registered voters

1214
00:48:16.400 --> 00:48:18.040
respond to a survey at all.

1215
00:48:18.260 --> 00:48:21.060
Weighting is not a technical adjustment applied at the end.

1216
00:48:21.220 --> 00:48:23.840
It is doing most of the work of producing the estimate.

1217
00:48:24.320 --> 00:48:26.360
Hillegus adds historical perspective.

1218
00:48:26.360 --> 00:48:30.420
She catalogues the error types, coverage, non-response,

1219
00:48:30.720 --> 00:48:32.020
measurement, processing,

1220
00:48:32.200 --> 00:48:34.900
and adjustment error, and reviews the famous failures.

1221
00:48:35.360 --> 00:48:36.000
1948.

1222
00:48:36.420 --> 00:48:39.680
Dewey defeats Truman, where pollsters stopped polling weeks

1223
00:48:39.680 --> 00:48:40.500
before the election.

1224
00:48:40.720 --> 00:48:41.240
2008.

1225
00:48:41.240 --> 00:48:44.880
In New Hampshire, where every poll had Obama winning and Clinton

1226
00:48:44.880 --> 00:48:45.200
won.

1227
00:48:45.360 --> 00:48:48.480
Her broader point is that polling error is not a modern crisis,

1228
00:48:48.820 --> 00:48:52.240
is a permanent feature that gets rediscovered every few cycles.

1229
00:48:52.760 --> 00:48:54.940
So what should you actually do with polls?

1230
00:48:54.940 --> 00:48:57.080
The advice from these readings is,

1231
00:48:57.280 --> 00:48:59.880
average many polls rather than trusting any single one,

1232
00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:01.700
but understand the limit of averaging.

1233
00:49:01.900 --> 00:49:03.280
It cancels random noise.

1234
00:49:03.460 --> 00:49:05.260
It does not cancel shared bias.

1235
00:49:05.560 --> 00:49:08.500
If every pollster is missing the same group in the same way,

1236
00:49:08.680 --> 00:49:10.360
which is what house effects are,

1237
00:49:10.540 --> 00:49:13.740
the average is exactly as wrong as the individual polls.

1238
00:49:13.920 --> 00:49:16.300
Treat the reported margin of error as a floor on your

1239
00:49:16.300 --> 00:49:16.840
uncertainty,

1240
00:49:17.140 --> 00:49:18.060
never as a ceiling.

1241
00:49:19.240 --> 00:49:20.320
Unit 11.

1242
00:49:20.680 --> 00:49:23.360
Understanding Probability and the Normal Distribution.

1243
00:49:23.720 --> 00:49:24.760
Lecture 21.

1244
00:49:25.200 --> 00:49:26.220
Pollock Chapter 8.

1245
00:49:26.480 --> 00:49:28.820
You need the normal curve for one reason.

1246
00:49:29.040 --> 00:49:31.520
It describes how sample statistics behave,

1247
00:49:31.520 --> 00:49:35.040
and that is what lets you say anything about a population from a

1248
00:49:35.040 --> 00:49:35.720
single sample.

1249
00:49:35.980 --> 00:49:39.080
The normal distribution is symmetric, bell-shaped,

1250
00:49:39.280 --> 00:49:41.400
and completely defined by two numbers,

1251
00:49:41.580 --> 00:49:43.100
its mean and its standard deviation.

1252
00:49:43.100 --> 00:49:45.020
The mean locates the center,

1253
00:49:45.260 --> 00:49:47.080
the standard deviation sets the width.

1254
00:49:47.360 --> 00:49:51.540
The rule to memorize is 68, 95, 99.7.

1255
00:49:51.880 --> 00:49:56.080
About 68% of cases fall within one standard deviation of the

1256
00:49:56.080 --> 00:49:56.360
mean.

1257
00:49:56.660 --> 00:49:59.740
About 95% fall within two standard deviations.

1258
00:50:00.040 --> 00:50:02.780
About 99.7% fall within three.

1259
00:50:03.020 --> 00:50:05.620
Picture the bell with three nested bands under it,

1260
00:50:05.800 --> 00:50:08.720
the narrow middle band holding roughly two-thirds of everything,

1261
00:50:08.980 --> 00:50:11.540
and the widest band holding essentially all of it.

1262
00:50:11.540 --> 00:50:14.680
The z-score converts any value onto that scale.

1263
00:50:14.860 --> 00:50:16.980
z equals the value minus the mean,

1264
00:50:17.140 --> 00:50:18.620
divided by the standard deviation.

1265
00:50:19.020 --> 00:50:22.580
A z-score of two means the case is two standard deviations above

1266
00:50:22.580 --> 00:50:23.040
the mean,

1267
00:50:23.260 --> 00:50:27.000
which puts it above roughly 97.5% of the distribution.

1268
00:50:27.660 --> 00:50:30.440
Now the concept that actually matters for inference,

1269
00:50:30.660 --> 00:50:32.000
the sampling distribution.

1270
00:50:32.360 --> 00:50:34.460
This is not the distribution of your data.

1271
00:50:34.720 --> 00:50:37.880
It is the distribution of a statistic across all the samples

1272
00:50:37.880 --> 00:50:39.560
you could have drawn from the population.

1273
00:50:39.560 --> 00:50:43.080
If you drew sample after sample and computed the mean each time,

1274
00:50:43.300 --> 00:50:46.200
those means would form a normal curve centered on the true

1275
00:50:46.200 --> 00:50:47.180
population value.

1276
00:50:47.560 --> 00:50:51.060
The standard deviation of that sampling distribution has its own

1277
00:50:51.060 --> 00:50:51.360
name,

1278
00:50:51.560 --> 00:50:52.560
the standard error.

1279
00:50:52.820 --> 00:50:55.460
It measures how much your estimate would bounce around from

1280
00:50:55.460 --> 00:50:56.380
sample to sample.

1281
00:50:56.560 --> 00:50:58.920
Larger samples give smaller standard errors,

1282
00:50:59.120 --> 00:51:01.820
which is the mathematical reason bigger samples are more

1283
00:51:01.820 --> 00:51:02.320
precise.

1284
00:51:02.320 --> 00:51:06.100
And the reason the sampling distribution is normal even when

1285
00:51:06.100 --> 00:51:07.820
your underlying variable is not

1286
00:51:07.820 --> 00:51:09.240
is the central limit theorem.

1287
00:51:09.560 --> 00:51:12.800
Sample means are approximately normally distributed as long as n

1288
00:51:12.800 --> 00:51:13.860
is decently large,

1289
00:51:14.020 --> 00:51:16.420
no matter how skewed the original variable is.

1290
00:51:16.560 --> 00:51:20.100
That theorem is why the normal curve shows up everywhere in this

1291
00:51:20.100 --> 00:51:20.400
course

1292
00:51:20.400 --> 00:51:23.440
even though almost nothing in politics is actually bell-shaped.

1293
00:51:24.580 --> 00:51:28.520
Unit 12. Statistical Significance and the Uses of Inference

1294
00:51:28.520 --> 00:51:31.920
Lectures 22-24 Pollock Chapter 9

1295
00:51:31.920 --> 00:51:33.580
This includes Quiz 5

1296
00:51:33.580 --> 00:51:35.280
You have a sample.

1297
00:51:35.500 --> 00:51:38.360
You want to say something about a population you cannot see.

1298
00:51:38.560 --> 00:51:41.220
Inference is the bridge and it comes in two forms.

1299
00:51:41.380 --> 00:51:44.340
An interval around your estimate and a test of whether a

1300
00:51:44.340 --> 00:51:45.360
relationship is real.

1301
00:51:45.840 --> 00:51:47.960
First, confidence intervals.

1302
00:51:47.960 --> 00:51:52.400
The formula is your estimate, plus or minus 1.96 times the

1303
00:51:52.400 --> 00:51:54.820
standard error for 95% confidence.

1304
00:51:55.420 --> 00:51:58.960
Now the interpretation, and this is the thing students get wrong

1305
00:51:58.960 --> 00:51:59.640
most often,

1306
00:51:59.860 --> 00:52:00.960
so listen carefully.

1307
00:52:01.500 --> 00:52:06.300
A 95% confidence interval does not mean there is a 95% chance

1308
00:52:06.300 --> 00:52:09.100
that the true value lies inside this particular interval.

1309
00:52:09.320 --> 00:52:10.620
The true value is fixed.

1310
00:52:10.840 --> 00:52:12.920
It is either in your interval or it is not.

1311
00:52:13.060 --> 00:52:14.800
There is no probability about it.

1312
00:52:14.960 --> 00:52:17.560
What is 95% is the procedure.

1313
00:52:17.960 --> 00:52:20.060
If you repeated this process over and over,

1314
00:52:20.480 --> 00:52:24.100
95% of the intervals you constructed would contain the true

1315
00:52:24.100 --> 00:52:24.540
value.

1316
00:52:24.980 --> 00:52:28.480
Picture 20 horizontal line segments stacked vertically,

1317
00:52:28.760 --> 00:52:31.020
each one an interval from a different sample,

1318
00:52:31.180 --> 00:52:34.180
and a single vertical line running down the picture marking the

1319
00:52:34.180 --> 00:52:34.840
true value.

1320
00:52:35.160 --> 00:52:38.020
19 of the 20 segments cross that vertical line.

1321
00:52:38.300 --> 00:52:41.240
One of them sits off to the side and misses it entirely.

1322
00:52:41.560 --> 00:52:44.120
That is what 95% confidence means.

1323
00:52:44.360 --> 00:52:47.220
It is a property of the method, not of any one interval.

1324
00:52:47.220 --> 00:52:50.280
Now hypothesis testing in four steps.

1325
00:52:50.600 --> 00:52:51.500
Step 1.

1326
00:52:51.720 --> 00:52:52.740
Date the null hypothesis.

1327
00:52:53.140 --> 00:52:55.640
There is no relationship between X and Y.

1328
00:52:56.080 --> 00:52:56.760
Step 2.

1329
00:52:57.040 --> 00:52:58.180
Assume the null is true.

1330
00:52:58.660 --> 00:52:59.460
Step 3.

1331
00:52:59.740 --> 00:53:00.100
Ask,

1332
00:53:00.300 --> 00:53:01.580
If the null were true,

1333
00:53:01.720 --> 00:53:04.520
how likely is it that I would see a relationship at least this

1334
00:53:04.520 --> 00:53:04.960
large,

1335
00:53:05.120 --> 00:53:06.500
purely from sampling luck?

1336
00:53:06.660 --> 00:53:08.520
That probability is the P value.

1337
00:53:08.820 --> 00:53:09.720
Step 4.

1338
00:53:09.720 --> 00:53:11.960
If P is less than 0.05,

1339
00:53:12.320 --> 00:53:13.640
reject the null hypothesis,

1340
00:53:13.660 --> 00:53:16.940
and describe the result as statistically significant.

1341
00:53:17.140 --> 00:53:19.220
If not, you fail to reject the null.

1342
00:53:19.740 --> 00:53:21.340
Note that phrasing carefully.

1343
00:53:21.580 --> 00:53:23.520
You never accept the null hypothesis.

1344
00:53:23.840 --> 00:53:25.160
You fail to reject it.

1345
00:53:25.400 --> 00:53:28.400
Failing to reject is not evidence that there is no relationship.

1346
00:53:28.760 --> 00:53:31.940
It may just mean your sample was too small to detect one.

1347
00:53:32.420 --> 00:53:35.220
Two kinds of error, arranged in a 2x2 table.

1348
00:53:35.220 --> 00:53:39.100
If in reality there is no relationship and you say there is one,

1349
00:53:39.300 --> 00:53:41.600
that is a type 1 error, a false positive.

1350
00:53:41.600 --> 00:53:43.860
You have announced something that is not there.

1351
00:53:44.040 --> 00:53:47.560
The 0.05 threshold is what caps that risk at 5%.

1352
00:53:47.560 --> 00:53:51.220
If in reality there is a relationship and you say there is none,

1353
00:53:51.480 --> 00:53:53.800
that is a type 2 error, a false negative,

1354
00:53:54.140 --> 00:53:56.320
usually caused by too small a sample.

1355
00:53:56.760 --> 00:53:58.900
The other two cells are correct answers.

1356
00:53:58.900 --> 00:54:03.300
Now, three things about significance that cost the most points.

1357
00:54:03.580 --> 00:54:07.440
First, statistically significant does not mean substantively

1358
00:54:07.440 --> 00:54:07.960
important.

1359
00:54:08.300 --> 00:54:09.520
With a large enough sample,

1360
00:54:09.840 --> 00:54:12.160
an utterly trivival effect becomes significant.

1361
00:54:12.480 --> 00:54:14.000
With 300,000 cases,

1362
00:54:14.340 --> 00:54:16.720
a difference of one-tenth of a percentage point

1363
00:54:16.720 --> 00:54:18.580
clears P less than 0.05

1364
00:54:18.580 --> 00:54:20.500
and means nothing in the real world.

1365
00:54:20.640 --> 00:54:22.360
Significance answers the question,

1366
00:54:22.580 --> 00:54:24.120
is this probably not zero?

1367
00:54:24.340 --> 00:54:25.760
It does not answer the question,

1368
00:54:26.180 --> 00:54:27.760
is this big enough to care about?

1369
00:54:27.760 --> 00:54:30.840
Second, small samples hide real effects,

1370
00:54:31.000 --> 00:54:34.460
so non-significant result is not proof of no relationship.

1371
00:54:34.780 --> 00:54:36.620
Third, and most important,

1372
00:54:36.960 --> 00:54:39.860
significance says nothing whatsoever about causality.

1373
00:54:40.140 --> 00:54:42.480
A statistically significant correlation still

1374
00:54:42.480 --> 00:54:44.320
has to clear all four hurdles.

1375
00:54:44.540 --> 00:54:48.480
Chocolate and Nobel Prizes was significant at P less than 0

1376
00:54:48.480 --> 00:54:49.920
.0001.

1377
00:54:50.140 --> 00:54:53.420
The model case for effect size versus significance

1378
00:54:53.420 --> 00:54:55.400
is levitt on capital punishment,

1379
00:54:55.400 --> 00:54:58.180
even if you accept the most generous published deterrence

1380
00:54:58.180 --> 00:54:58.640
estimates.

1381
00:54:59.060 --> 00:55:02.960
Executions account for roughly 1.5% of the 1990s homicide

1382
00:55:02.960 --> 00:55:03.640
decline.

1383
00:55:03.860 --> 00:55:05.320
The effect might be real.

1384
00:55:05.560 --> 00:55:08.240
It is nowhere near large enough to be that explanation.

1385
00:55:08.580 --> 00:55:10.320
Real, arguably significant,

1386
00:55:10.340 --> 00:55:11.980
and far too small to matter.

1387
00:55:12.180 --> 00:55:15.380
That distinction is exactly what a good exam answer draws.

1388
00:55:16.560 --> 00:55:20.580
Unit 13, exploring causal relationships with correlation and

1389
00:55:20.580 --> 00:55:21.000
regression.

1390
00:55:21.420 --> 00:55:23.680
Lecture 28, Pollack Chapter 11.

1391
00:55:24.180 --> 00:55:25.500
Tart with the stator plot.

1392
00:55:25.680 --> 00:55:26.940
Each dot is one case,

1393
00:55:27.120 --> 00:55:30.780
positioned by its value on x horizontally and its value on y

1394
00:55:30.780 --> 00:55:31.180
vertically.

1395
00:55:31.960 --> 00:55:35.140
Correlation puts a single number on how tightly the dots follow

1396
00:55:35.140 --> 00:55:35.900
a straight line.

1397
00:55:36.200 --> 00:55:38.980
Pearson's R runs from negative 1 to positive 1.

1398
00:55:38.980 --> 00:55:40.400
The sign tells you direction.

1399
00:55:40.720 --> 00:55:42.440
The magnitude tells you strength.

1400
00:55:42.760 --> 00:55:46.920
An R of about positive 0.9 looks like a tight upward band of

1401
00:55:46.920 --> 00:55:47.260
dots.

1402
00:55:47.560 --> 00:55:51.620
An R of about positive 0.45 looks like a loose upward cloud,

1403
00:55:51.820 --> 00:55:53.220
where you can see the tendency,

1404
00:55:53.640 --> 00:55:55.280
but plenty of dots violate it.

1405
00:55:55.400 --> 00:55:57.680
An R of 0 looks like a shapeless blob.

1406
00:55:57.920 --> 00:56:02.140
An R of negative 0.75 is a fairly tight downward band.

1407
00:56:02.480 --> 00:56:03.880
Two cautions on R.

1408
00:56:03.880 --> 00:56:07.300
First, R equals 0 means no straight line relationship.

1409
00:56:07.580 --> 00:56:11.080
A perfect U shape has an R near 0 even though the relationship

1410
00:56:11.080 --> 00:56:11.800
is perfect.

1411
00:56:12.020 --> 00:56:14.160
Correlation only detects linear patterns.

1412
00:56:14.520 --> 00:56:18.280
Second, R squared is the proportion of the variation in Y that X

1413
00:56:18.280 --> 00:56:18.880
accounts for.

1414
00:56:19.060 --> 00:56:22.180
Chocolate and Nobel prices had an R of 0.79,

1415
00:56:22.180 --> 00:56:24.880
so R squared is about 0.62,

1416
00:56:25.320 --> 00:56:29.500
meaning chocolate consumption statistically accounts for 62% of

1417
00:56:29.500 --> 00:56:30.880
the variation in Nobel rates.

1418
00:56:30.880 --> 00:56:33.880
That sounds enormous and is still not remotely causal.

1419
00:56:34.400 --> 00:56:35.200
Now regression.

1420
00:56:35.500 --> 00:56:37.320
Regression gives you a prediction rule,

1421
00:56:37.520 --> 00:56:38.720
a line through the scatter.

1422
00:56:38.980 --> 00:56:41.440
The equation is Y equals a plus speed X.

1423
00:56:41.900 --> 00:56:43.240
The letter A is the intercept,

1424
00:56:43.640 --> 00:56:46.700
which is the predicted value of Y when X is 0.

1425
00:56:47.040 --> 00:56:49.600
Sometimes that is meaningful and sometimes it is not.

1426
00:56:50.040 --> 00:56:51.520
The letter B is the slope,

1427
00:56:51.700 --> 00:56:53.880
and B is the thing exams ask about.

1428
00:56:54.060 --> 00:56:54.780
Picture the line,

1429
00:56:54.960 --> 00:56:57.380
and picture a small right triangle drawn under it.

1430
00:56:57.500 --> 00:56:59.460
Move one unit to the right along X,

1431
00:56:59.460 --> 00:57:00.320
that is the run,

1432
00:57:00.460 --> 00:57:01.820
and the line rises by B,

1433
00:57:02.120 --> 00:57:03.100
that is the rise.

1434
00:57:03.280 --> 00:57:04.660
Slope is rise over run.

1435
00:57:04.960 --> 00:57:07.420
Here is the sentence to write on the exam.

1436
00:57:07.600 --> 00:57:11.600
A one unit increase in X is associated with a change of B units

1437
00:57:11.600 --> 00:57:12.060
in Y.

1438
00:57:12.400 --> 00:57:14.500
Notice I said associated with,

1439
00:57:14.680 --> 00:57:15.420
not causes.

1440
00:57:15.780 --> 00:57:18.340
The slope is a description of the pattern in the data.

1441
00:57:18.640 --> 00:57:20.900
Causality still has to clear the four hurdles.

1442
00:57:21.480 --> 00:57:22.740
Now multiple regression,

1443
00:57:23.100 --> 00:57:25.280
which is where statistical control lives.

1444
00:57:25.580 --> 00:57:26.720
In a multiple regression,

1445
00:57:26.720 --> 00:57:30.600
each coefficient is the effect of that variable holding all the

1446
00:57:30.600 --> 00:57:32.340
other variables in the model constant.

1447
00:57:32.580 --> 00:57:33.200
That phrase,

1448
00:57:33.440 --> 00:57:35.320
holding the other variables constant,

1449
00:57:35.580 --> 00:57:38.120
should appear in every regression answer you write.

1450
00:57:38.280 --> 00:57:40.720
It is why regression tables have so many rows.

1451
00:57:41.020 --> 00:57:43.980
Each additional row is a confounder the author is trying to rule

1452
00:57:43.980 --> 00:57:44.260
out.

1453
00:57:44.820 --> 00:57:46.220
Reading a regression table,

1454
00:57:46.460 --> 00:57:47.680
each row is a variable.

1455
00:57:47.920 --> 00:57:49.620
The first number is the coefficient,

1456
00:57:49.960 --> 00:57:50.120
B,

1457
00:57:50.400 --> 00:57:52.300
which gives you direction and size.

1458
00:57:52.480 --> 00:57:53.700
Below it or beside it,

1459
00:57:53.700 --> 00:57:55.180
usually in parentheses,

1460
00:57:55.680 --> 00:57:57.100
is the standard error,

1461
00:57:57.340 --> 00:58:00.620
which tells you how precisely that coefficient is estimated.

1462
00:58:00.940 --> 00:58:01.460
Asterisks,

1463
00:58:01.480 --> 00:58:02.740
or a separate column,

1464
00:58:03.140 --> 00:58:04.160
indicate significance.

1465
00:58:04.820 --> 00:58:06.300
A useful rule of thumb,

1466
00:58:06.480 --> 00:58:09.440
if the coefficient is more than about twice its standard error,

1467
00:58:09.580 --> 00:58:11.820
it is significant at the .05 level.

1468
00:58:11.980 --> 00:58:15.020
That lets you read significance straight off a table without a p

1469
00:58:15.020 --> 00:58:15.340
column.

1470
00:58:15.800 --> 00:58:17.400
Final warnings on regression.

1471
00:58:17.760 --> 00:58:19.220
Correlation is not causation.

1472
00:58:19.440 --> 00:58:21.760
No matter how many controls are in the model,

1473
00:58:21.760 --> 00:58:24.360
outliers can drive a line all by themselves.

1474
00:58:24.700 --> 00:58:26.080
So look at the scatterplot.

1475
00:58:26.200 --> 00:58:28.580
Do not extrapolate beyond the range of your data,

1476
00:58:28.580 --> 00:58:31.660
and the whole apparatus assumes the relationship is linear,

1477
00:58:31.960 --> 00:58:34.620
which is an assumption you should check rather than assume.

1478
00:58:35.900 --> 00:58:37.020
Unit 14.

1479
00:58:37.340 --> 00:58:39.080
Causal Inference in Small Numbers.

1480
00:58:39.280 --> 00:58:40.060
Lecture 30.

1481
00:58:40.360 --> 00:58:40.680
King,

1482
00:58:41.120 --> 00:58:42.120
Cohen and Verba,

1483
00:58:42.320 --> 00:58:44.760
and the Frickonomics Chapter on Drug Dealers.

1484
00:58:45.020 --> 00:58:47.240
When you have only a handful of cases,

1485
00:58:47.500 --> 00:58:50.300
you cannot rely on randomness to sort things out.

1486
00:58:50.300 --> 00:58:52.260
You have to choose your cases deliberately,

1487
00:58:52.320 --> 00:58:53.200
and King,

1488
00:58:53.600 --> 00:58:56.760
Cohen and Verba give you one rule that decides most of it.

1489
00:58:57.060 --> 00:59:00.280
Select your observations on the basis of the explanatory

1490
00:59:00.280 --> 00:59:00.760
variable,

1491
00:59:01.100 --> 00:59:02.600
never on the dependent variable,

1492
00:59:02.780 --> 00:59:03.800
and never on both.

1493
00:59:04.240 --> 00:59:05.780
Let me unpack why.

1494
00:59:06.400 --> 00:59:08.360
Selecting on the explanatory variable,

1495
00:59:08.660 --> 00:59:09.000
X,

1496
00:59:09.200 --> 00:59:11.260
is legitimate and often desirable.

1497
00:59:11.540 --> 00:59:15.020
Deliberately choosing cases with extreme or widely varied values

1498
00:59:15.020 --> 00:59:15.460
of X

1499
00:59:15.460 --> 00:59:16.640
increases your leverage.

1500
00:59:16.640 --> 00:59:20.440
The only cost is that your findings apply to that range of X and

1501
00:59:20.440 --> 00:59:21.220
not beyond it.

1502
00:59:21.700 --> 00:59:24.540
Selecting on the dependent variable is the cardinal sin,

1503
00:59:24.740 --> 00:59:26.360
and it comes in two flavors.

1504
00:59:26.700 --> 00:59:29.840
The severe version is no validation in Y at all.

1505
00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:32.940
If you study twenty civil wars in order to learn what causes

1506
00:59:32.940 --> 00:59:33.660
civil wars,

1507
00:59:33.880 --> 00:59:34.820
you can learn nothing.

1508
00:59:35.120 --> 00:59:36.780
Every case has the same outcome,

1509
00:59:37.000 --> 00:59:39.600
so nothing distinguishes causes from mere constants.

1510
00:59:39.920 --> 00:59:43.040
You never observe the countries that had similar conditions and

1511
00:59:43.040 --> 00:59:43.980
stayed at peace.

1512
00:59:43.980 --> 00:59:46.320
The design is what they call indeterminate.

1513
00:59:46.460 --> 00:59:48.900
Same problem if you study only successful companies,

1514
00:59:49.240 --> 00:59:50.260
only school shooters,

1515
00:59:50.540 --> 00:59:52.300
or only high-achieving students.

1516
00:59:52.540 --> 00:59:54.560
The subtler version is truncation.

1517
00:59:54.840 --> 00:59:56.960
Suppose you keep only the high end of Y,

1518
00:59:57.300 --> 00:59:57.980
not all of it.

1519
00:59:58.120 --> 01:00:00.120
Now you do not get zero information.

1520
01:00:00.180 --> 01:00:01.800
You get a biased estimate.

1521
01:00:02.020 --> 01:00:02.480
Specifically,

1522
01:00:03.060 --> 01:00:06.220
truncating on Y biases they estimated affect toward zero.

1523
01:00:06.440 --> 01:00:08.860
It makes X look weaker than it really is.

1524
01:00:09.340 --> 01:00:12.620
Picture a scatterplot with a clear upward slope through all the

1525
01:00:12.620 --> 01:00:12.980
cases.

1526
01:00:12.980 --> 01:00:16.700
Now draw a horizontal line partway up and throw away everything

1527
01:00:16.700 --> 01:00:17.240
below it.

1528
01:00:17.340 --> 01:00:19.280
Fit a new line through only the survivors.

1529
01:00:19.580 --> 01:00:21.480
That new line is noticeably flatter.

1530
01:00:21.740 --> 01:00:22.300
Same data,

1531
01:00:22.540 --> 01:00:24.100
same underlying relationship,

1532
01:00:24.380 --> 01:00:26.040
roughly half the estimated effect.

1533
01:00:26.520 --> 01:00:27.980
There is one consolation,

1534
01:00:28.220 --> 01:00:30.240
because the direction of the bias is known.

1535
01:00:30.460 --> 01:00:33.280
If you find a significant effect in a truncated sample,

1536
01:00:33.520 --> 01:00:35.780
the true effect must be at least that large.

1537
01:00:35.920 --> 01:00:37.540
So a positive finding survives,

1538
01:00:37.820 --> 01:00:39.060
a null finding does not.

1539
01:00:39.580 --> 01:00:42.140
Selecting on both X and Y is the worst case.

1540
01:00:42.140 --> 01:00:45.560
The bias can run in any direction and its magnitude is

1541
01:00:45.560 --> 01:00:46.040
unknowable.

1542
01:00:46.260 --> 01:00:46.840
Effectively,

1543
01:00:46.960 --> 01:00:48.880
you can manufacture any result you want.

1544
01:00:49.060 --> 01:00:52.080
And here is the trap that catches careful people.

1545
01:00:52.340 --> 01:00:56.160
Any selection rule that is correlated with Y induces this bias,

1546
01:00:56.400 --> 01:00:59.060
even one you never thought of as selecting on the outcome.

1547
01:00:59.240 --> 01:01:01.800
Using only cases that were covered in newspapers.

1548
01:01:02.140 --> 01:01:04.040
Only cases considered important.

1549
01:01:04.440 --> 01:01:06.920
Only cases where data happens to be available.

1550
01:01:07.240 --> 01:01:10.320
Only organizations that survived long enough to be studied.

1551
01:01:10.320 --> 01:01:13.160
All of those are selection on the dependent variable in

1552
01:01:13.160 --> 01:01:13.580
disguise.

1553
01:01:14.020 --> 01:01:15.140
One more point.

1554
01:01:15.400 --> 01:01:18.140
Random selection is the default for large N research,

1555
01:01:18.460 --> 01:01:20.240
but it is dangerous for small N.

1556
01:01:20.480 --> 01:01:21.400
With six cases,

1557
01:01:21.620 --> 01:01:25.180
a random draw can easily produce a wildly unrepresentative set.

1558
01:01:25.320 --> 01:01:26.900
That is precisely why King,

1559
01:01:27.420 --> 01:01:29.480
Kenohane, and Verber recommend intentional,

1560
01:01:29.780 --> 01:01:32.060
deliberate selection on X for qualitative work.

1561
01:01:32.240 --> 01:01:34.840
The practical checklist for a small N design.

1562
01:01:35.120 --> 01:01:37.060
Make sure there is varination in Y.

1563
01:01:37.060 --> 01:01:39.460
Make sure there is varination in the key decks.

1564
01:01:39.940 --> 01:01:41.500
Make the selection rule explicit.

1565
01:01:41.760 --> 01:01:44.780
And then ask yourself honestly whether that rule could be

1566
01:01:44.780 --> 01:01:45.940
correlated with the outcome.

1567
01:01:46.180 --> 01:01:48.920
The Freakonomics chapter is paired with this reading.

1568
01:01:49.260 --> 01:01:52.220
Sudhir Venkatesh obtained a Chicago crack gang's actual

1569
01:01:52.220 --> 01:01:53.240
financial records,

1570
01:01:53.400 --> 01:01:56.400
which showed foot soldiers earning below minimum wage,

1571
01:01:56.580 --> 01:01:58.500
while the gang leader took a large share,

1572
01:01:58.700 --> 01:02:01.320
which is why so many dealers still lived with their mothers.

1573
01:02:01.320 --> 01:02:05.140
What a single case like that can do is demonstrate a mechanism

1574
01:02:05.140 --> 01:02:06.480
and destroy a stereotype.

1575
01:02:06.840 --> 01:02:08.200
Those are real contributions.

1576
01:02:08.600 --> 01:02:11.460
What it cannot do is establish a general causal claim.

1577
01:02:11.640 --> 01:02:13.640
One non-randomly obtained case.

1578
01:02:13.860 --> 01:02:15.100
No comparison group.

1579
01:02:15.320 --> 01:02:16.820
No varination in the outcome.

1580
01:02:17.120 --> 01:02:19.180
Read it for what a single case is good for,

1581
01:02:19.340 --> 01:02:20.360
and what it is not.

1582
01:02:21.640 --> 01:02:24.400
Now let me take you through the seven debates the course is

1583
01:02:24.400 --> 01:02:24.980
built around.

1584
01:02:25.200 --> 01:02:26.860
For each one I will give you the claim,

1585
01:02:27.040 --> 01:02:27.640
the test,

1586
01:02:27.860 --> 01:02:28.340
the verdict,

1587
01:02:28.540 --> 01:02:29.560
and the methods lesson.

1588
01:02:29.560 --> 01:02:30.740
Debate 1.

1589
01:02:30.900 --> 01:02:32.160
What's the matter with Kansas?

1590
01:02:32.460 --> 01:02:33.500
Lecture 5.

1591
01:02:33.780 --> 01:02:34.720
The claim,

1592
01:02:34.900 --> 01:02:35.820
from Thomas Frank,

1593
01:02:36.120 --> 01:02:39.160
working class whites are voting against their economic interests

1594
01:02:39.160 --> 01:02:42.560
because the Republican Party mobilizes them on cultural issues.

1595
01:02:42.820 --> 01:02:45.700
His headline evidence is that the poorest county in America

1596
01:02:45.700 --> 01:02:48.220
went for Bush by over 75 percent.

1597
01:02:48.880 --> 01:02:49.460
The test,

1598
01:02:49.860 --> 01:02:50.240
Bartels,

1599
01:02:50.500 --> 01:02:53.900
using 14 elections of American National Election Study survey

1600
01:02:53.900 --> 01:02:54.260
data

1601
01:02:54.260 --> 01:02:56.280
with region and income controlled,

1602
01:02:56.480 --> 01:02:57.080
and Rafini,

1603
01:02:57.080 --> 01:02:59.840
asking the same question two decades later.

1604
01:03:00.100 --> 01:03:01.000
The verdict,

1605
01:03:01.260 --> 01:03:02.840
the thesis does not survive,

1606
01:03:03.080 --> 01:03:05.040
minus 5.9 points overall,

1607
01:03:05.500 --> 01:03:07.560
minus 19.7 in the South,

1608
01:03:07.940 --> 01:03:09.800
minus 1.0 outside it,

1609
01:03:09.940 --> 01:03:10.460
by income,

1610
01:03:10.740 --> 01:03:14.340
poorer whites move toward the Democrats by 4.5 points,

1611
01:03:14.680 --> 01:03:17.180
economic issues outweighed abortion and vote choice,

1612
01:03:17.460 --> 01:03:18.720
by more than 2 to 1,

1613
01:03:18.940 --> 01:03:19.640
and abortion,

1614
01:03:19.880 --> 01:03:23.700
ranked 13th of 15 issues for non-college whites in 2004.

1615
01:03:23.700 --> 01:03:25.260
Methods lesson,

1616
01:03:25.500 --> 01:03:27.780
operationalization decides the answer,

1617
01:03:28.020 --> 01:03:29.680
plus the ecological fallacy,

1618
01:03:29.880 --> 01:03:31.760
and controlling for a lurking variable.

1619
01:03:32.160 --> 01:03:32.980
Debate 2,

1620
01:03:33.220 --> 01:03:34.660
who receives public assistance,

1621
01:03:35.020 --> 01:03:35.680
Lecture 7.

1622
01:03:36.140 --> 01:03:36.780
The claim,

1623
01:03:37.200 --> 01:03:40.280
Americans oppose welfare because of how they picture the poor,

1624
01:03:40.440 --> 01:03:42.020
and that picture is racialized.

1625
01:03:42.400 --> 01:03:43.120
The test,

1626
01:03:43.680 --> 01:03:45.760
Martin Jilins uses content analysis,

1627
01:03:45.760 --> 01:03:49.260
he codes the racial composition of poor people shown in news

1628
01:03:49.260 --> 01:03:52.140
magazine photographs and in network television coverage,

1629
01:03:52.340 --> 01:03:55.880
and compares that against the actual composition of the poverty

1630
01:03:55.880 --> 01:03:57.000
population in census,

1631
01:03:57.300 --> 01:03:59.360
and current population survey data.

1632
01:03:59.700 --> 01:04:00.500
The verdict,

1633
01:04:00.720 --> 01:04:03.860
the media portrait is systematically unrepresentative of the

1634
01:04:03.860 --> 01:04:05.100
real poverty population,

1635
01:04:05.440 --> 01:04:08.480
and public misperception tracks the portrait rather than the

1636
01:04:08.480 --> 01:04:09.380
underlying data.

1637
01:04:09.940 --> 01:04:10.960
Methods lesson,

1638
01:04:10.960 --> 01:04:13.460
Content analysis as a measurement strategy,

1639
01:04:13.720 --> 01:04:16.380
and the design of comparing a measured perception against a

1640
01:04:16.380 --> 01:04:17.180
measured reality.

1641
01:04:17.580 --> 01:04:17.840
Also,

1642
01:04:18.120 --> 01:04:20.840
where public opinion data comes from in the first place.

1643
01:04:21.240 --> 01:04:22.140
Debate 3,

1644
01:04:22.420 --> 01:04:24.560
Is Stop and Frisk Racial Profiling?

1645
01:04:24.760 --> 01:04:25.460
Lecture 9.

1646
01:04:25.700 --> 01:04:26.620
The claim,

1647
01:04:26.780 --> 01:04:28.860
from Michael Bloomberg in the Washington Post,

1648
01:04:29.160 --> 01:04:31.840
stop rates should be benchmarked against the descriptions of

1649
01:04:31.840 --> 01:04:32.780
criminal suspects,

1650
01:04:33.140 --> 01:04:35.020
not against the population at large,

1651
01:04:35.180 --> 01:04:38.740
so the racial disparity in stops is not evidence of profiling.

1652
01:04:38.740 --> 01:04:39.920
The test,

1653
01:04:40.380 --> 01:04:43.680
Bertrand and Mullenathan stop arguing about which denominator is

1654
01:04:43.680 --> 01:04:44.040
correct.

1655
01:04:44.340 --> 01:04:45.720
They randomly assign race,

1656
01:04:45.960 --> 01:04:46.780
via the name,

1657
01:04:47.000 --> 01:04:50.720
to otherwise identical resumes and mail out 5,000 of them.

1658
01:04:51.180 --> 01:04:51.780
The verdict,

1659
01:04:52.200 --> 01:04:57.440
9.65% callbacks for white-sounding names against 6.45% for

1660
01:04:57.440 --> 01:04:59.520
African-American-sounding names,

1661
01:04:59.720 --> 01:05:00.980
a 50% gap,

1662
01:05:01.220 --> 01:05:02.980
worth about eight years of experience,

1663
01:05:03.280 --> 01:05:05.280
and it widens as resumes improve.

1664
01:05:05.880 --> 01:05:06.840
Methods lesson,

1665
01:05:06.840 --> 01:05:10.160
The entire argument in the op-ed is about choosing a comparison

1666
01:05:10.160 --> 01:05:10.560
group.

1667
01:05:10.740 --> 01:05:13.560
An experiment constructs the comparison group by design,

1668
01:05:13.760 --> 01:05:16.100
which is why it settles what the op-ed cannot.

1669
01:05:16.680 --> 01:05:17.560
Debate 4,

1670
01:05:17.920 --> 01:05:19.060
Does Money Buy Votes?

1671
01:05:19.260 --> 01:05:20.020
Lecture 11.

1672
01:05:20.400 --> 01:05:21.340
The claim,

1673
01:05:21.640 --> 01:05:24.580
Campaign contributions correlate with roll-call votes,

1674
01:05:24.780 --> 01:05:26.540
therefore donors are buying legislators.

1675
01:05:27.100 --> 01:05:27.840
The test,

1676
01:05:28.120 --> 01:05:30.920
John Wright points out that the correlation is a weak design,

1677
01:05:31.160 --> 01:05:34.300
because donors give to legislators who already agree with them,

1678
01:05:34.300 --> 01:05:36.380
so the causation may run backwards.

1679
01:05:36.600 --> 01:05:39.780
Then Hertel Fernandez and colleagues take a different approach.

1680
01:05:39.980 --> 01:05:44.060
They survey 101 senior congressional staffers from 91 offices,

1681
01:05:44.380 --> 01:05:47.780
and compare the staffers' beliefs about constituent opinion

1682
01:05:47.780 --> 01:05:51.180
against actual district-level estimates from large survey data.

1683
01:05:51.640 --> 01:05:52.340
The verdict,

1684
01:05:52.340 --> 01:05:56.820
staffers misjudge their constituents badly and systematically in

1685
01:05:56.820 --> 01:05:57.980
a conservative direction.

1686
01:05:58.160 --> 01:06:01.820
91% underestimated constituent support for gun background

1687
01:06:01.820 --> 01:06:02.360
checks,

1688
01:06:02.580 --> 01:06:06.580
78% underestimated support for limits on carbon dioxide

1689
01:06:06.580 --> 01:06:07.140
emissions,

1690
01:06:07.320 --> 01:06:08.400
and a list experiment,

1691
01:06:08.680 --> 01:06:11.200
which is a technique for asking embarrassing questions

1692
01:06:11.200 --> 01:06:11.560
anonymously,

1693
01:06:12.180 --> 01:06:16.040
found that 45% had developed a new perspective on an issue after

1694
01:06:16.040 --> 01:06:18.840
speaking with a group that made campaign contributions.

1695
01:06:18.840 --> 01:06:21.820
So the channel is not buying votes directly,

1696
01:06:21.820 --> 01:06:25.100
it is shaping what legislators believe their voters want.

1697
01:06:25.680 --> 01:06:26.540
Methods lessen,

1698
01:06:26.700 --> 01:06:27.720
reverse causation,

1699
01:06:27.940 --> 01:06:28.940
which is hurdle 2,

1700
01:06:29.140 --> 01:06:31.080
the list experiment as a measurement technique,

1701
01:06:31.580 --> 01:06:34.480
and the difference between establishing that an effect exists

1702
01:06:34.480 --> 01:06:37.040
and identifying the mechanism that produces it.

1703
01:06:37.540 --> 01:06:38.420
Debate 5.

1704
01:06:38.620 --> 01:06:39.240
Who votes,

1705
01:06:39.400 --> 01:06:40.540
and is it getting worse?

1706
01:06:40.760 --> 01:06:41.720
Lecture 14.

1707
01:06:42.360 --> 01:06:42.920
The claim,

1708
01:06:43.240 --> 01:06:44.220
Turnout is falling,

1709
01:06:44.220 --> 01:06:46.220
and as economic inequality grows,

1710
01:06:46.420 --> 01:06:49.380
the electorate is becoming richer relative to the country as a

1711
01:06:49.380 --> 01:06:49.680
whole.

1712
01:06:50.180 --> 01:06:50.880
The test,

1713
01:06:51.280 --> 01:06:52.160
Lili and Nagler,

1714
01:06:52.340 --> 01:06:56.640
using current population survey data from 1972 to 2008,

1715
01:06:56.920 --> 01:06:58.080
with representativeness,

1716
01:06:58.260 --> 01:06:58.780
ratio lows,

1717
01:06:58.860 --> 01:06:59.800
and relative measures.

1718
01:07:00.020 --> 01:07:00.920
The verdict,

1719
01:07:01.320 --> 01:07:02.420
both claims fail,

1720
01:07:02.700 --> 01:07:07.200
turnout ran between 58 and 66% with no trend once you use,

1721
01:07:07.340 --> 01:07:08.800
citizens as the denominator,

1722
01:07:09.040 --> 01:07:13.420
and income bias in turnout was identical in 1972 and 2008.

1723
01:07:13.420 --> 01:07:14.900
Methods lessen,

1724
01:07:15.080 --> 01:07:16.700
denominators change conclusions,

1725
01:07:17.120 --> 01:07:19.700
relative versus absolute measures over time,

1726
01:07:19.880 --> 01:07:22.200
and knowing when to control and when not to.

1727
01:07:22.660 --> 01:07:23.560
Debate 6.

1728
01:07:23.980 --> 01:07:25.180
Are Independents Rising?

1729
01:07:25.420 --> 01:07:26.240
Lecture 20.

1730
01:07:26.500 --> 01:07:27.440
The claim,

1731
01:07:27.640 --> 01:07:30.960
From Huma Khan at ABC News and David Brooks in the New York

1732
01:07:30.960 --> 01:07:31.380
Times,

1733
01:07:31.640 --> 01:07:35.000
a record 40% of Americans now identify as independent,

1734
01:07:35.420 --> 01:07:38.220
making them the largest and most decisive bloc in American

1735
01:07:38.220 --> 01:07:38.840
politics.

1736
01:07:39.120 --> 01:07:40.100
The test,

1737
01:07:40.420 --> 01:07:41.580
Bruce Keith and colleagues,

1738
01:07:41.580 --> 01:07:43.520
in the myth of the independent voter,

1739
01:07:43.700 --> 01:07:47.020
push self-identified independence with a follow-up question

1740
01:07:47.020 --> 01:07:48.720
about which party they lean toward.

1741
01:07:49.020 --> 01:07:50.700
Then Anthony Fowler and colleagues,

1742
01:07:50.920 --> 01:07:54.640
in a 2023 American Political Science review article,

1743
01:07:55.280 --> 01:07:58.940
model more than 280,000 survey respondents' actual positions

1744
01:07:58.940 --> 01:08:00.980
across 20-plus policy questions,

1745
01:08:01.220 --> 01:08:03.280
rather than trusting a self-applied label.

1746
01:08:03.500 --> 01:08:04.380
The verdict,

1747
01:08:04.380 --> 01:08:07.420
most so-called independents are closet partisans.

1748
01:08:07.640 --> 01:08:09.000
They lean toward a party,

1749
01:08:09.100 --> 01:08:12.060
and then they vote for it at rates close to weak partisans.

1750
01:08:12.220 --> 01:08:15.820
Alan Abramowitz estimates that genuinely uncommitted voters

1751
01:08:15.820 --> 01:08:18.500
are less than 10% of actual voters,

1752
01:08:18.640 --> 01:08:22.280
and Fowler and colleagues find that about 73% of Americans

1753
01:08:22.280 --> 01:08:25.920
are well described by a single left-right ideological dimension,

1754
01:08:26.200 --> 01:08:29.640
about 21% hold genuine but idiosyncratic positions,

1755
01:08:29.640 --> 01:08:33.520
and about 6.5% are simply answering inattentively.

1756
01:08:33.760 --> 01:08:36.600
Here is the finding that makes that paper worth knowing.

1757
01:08:37.100 --> 01:08:40.100
Inattentive respondents give more extreme answers than actual

1758
01:08:40.100 --> 01:08:41.120
extremists do.

1759
01:08:41.520 --> 01:08:44.480
Random noise in survey responses looks like extremism,

1760
01:08:44.680 --> 01:08:47.480
which means measurement error has been inflating estimates of

1761
01:08:47.480 --> 01:08:48.080
polarization.

1762
01:08:48.760 --> 01:08:49.560
Methods lesson.

1763
01:08:49.780 --> 01:08:53.040
A self-reported label is not the same thing as the concept,

1764
01:08:53.200 --> 01:08:56.520
and measurement error can masquerade as a real category of

1765
01:08:56.520 --> 01:08:56.860
people.

1766
01:08:56.860 --> 01:08:58.200
Debate 7.

1767
01:08:58.460 --> 01:09:00.760
Why did crime fall in the 1990s?

1768
01:09:00.900 --> 01:09:01.860
Lecture 25.

1769
01:09:02.340 --> 01:09:03.300
The claim,

1770
01:09:03.460 --> 01:09:05.320
from Malcolm Gladwell in The New Yorker,

1771
01:09:05.580 --> 01:09:07.860
crime behaves like an epidemic with a tipping point,

1772
01:09:08.020 --> 01:09:10.660
and broken windows policing tip New York City.

1773
01:09:10.880 --> 01:09:11.840
The test.

1774
01:09:12.120 --> 01:09:14.560
Stephen Levitt takes the best-identified estimate

1775
01:09:14.560 --> 01:09:16.980
for each proposed cause and multiplies it

1776
01:09:16.980 --> 01:09:19.180
by how much that factor actually changed.

1777
01:09:19.420 --> 01:09:21.140
Robert Sampson and Alex Winter

1778
01:09:21.140 --> 01:09:23.820
follow a Chicago birth cohort for 18 years,

1779
01:09:24.120 --> 01:09:25.820
triangulating ordinary regression,

1780
01:09:25.820 --> 01:09:26.460
matching,

1781
01:09:26.720 --> 01:09:28.100
and instrumental variables

1782
01:09:28.100 --> 01:09:30.880
to test whether childhood lead exposure caused crime.

1783
01:09:31.060 --> 01:09:31.920
The verdict.

1784
01:09:32.280 --> 01:09:34.460
Homicide fell 43% nationally,

1785
01:09:34.720 --> 01:09:35.560
and it fell everywhere,

1786
01:09:35.900 --> 01:09:36.920
not just in New York.

1787
01:09:37.080 --> 01:09:39.440
San Diego fell 72.8%.

1788
01:09:39.440 --> 01:09:41.600
Austin fell 69.5%.

1789
01:09:42.200 --> 01:09:44.500
San Jose fell 69.2%.

1790
01:09:44.500 --> 01:09:46.600
New York fell 73.6%.

1791
01:09:47.160 --> 01:09:49.700
And New York expanded its police force

1792
01:09:49.700 --> 01:09:51.660
at roughly three times the national rate.

1793
01:09:51.800 --> 01:09:52.960
Once you adjust for that,

1794
01:09:53.280 --> 01:09:55.140
New York's decline is close to average.

1795
01:09:55.140 --> 01:09:56.800
It was not a special case,

1796
01:09:57.020 --> 01:09:59.100
and it did not need a special explanation.

1797
01:09:59.720 --> 01:10:02.620
Sampson and Winter find that childhood lead exposure

1798
01:10:02.620 --> 01:10:04.120
raises antisocial behavior,

1799
01:10:04.360 --> 01:10:06.520
but shows no direct effect on arrests,

1800
01:10:06.720 --> 01:10:09.680
and they are careful to warn that individual level results

1801
01:10:09.680 --> 01:10:11.440
do not settle the aggregate question.

1802
01:10:11.980 --> 01:10:13.000
Methods Lesson

1803
01:10:13.000 --> 01:10:14.700
Effect Size Accounting

1804
01:10:14.700 --> 01:10:17.020
A Mechanism Story is not a Test,

1805
01:10:17.220 --> 01:10:20.020
and triangulating several methods that rest on different

1806
01:10:20.020 --> 01:10:20.520
assumptions.

1807
01:10:21.840 --> 01:10:24.640
Let me spend one more minute on Levitt's accounting,

1808
01:10:24.860 --> 01:10:27.160
because it is the best illustration in the course of the

1809
01:10:27.160 --> 01:10:29.960
difference between an effect existing and an effect mattering.

1810
01:10:29.960 --> 01:10:33.100
Levitt takes each proposed cause of the crime decline,

1811
01:10:33.460 --> 01:10:35.980
uses the best available estimate of its effect,

1812
01:10:36.520 --> 01:10:40.300
multiplies by how much that factor actually changed between 1991

1813
01:10:40.300 --> 01:10:41.520
and 2001,

1814
01:10:41.840 --> 01:10:43.660
and sees what is left over.

1815
01:10:43.960 --> 01:10:45.580
Four factors explain it.

1816
01:10:45.580 --> 01:10:47.200
The rising prison population,

1817
01:10:47.440 --> 01:10:49.860
about 12% of the homicide decline.

1818
01:10:50.180 --> 01:10:51.220
Legalized abortion,

1819
01:10:51.620 --> 01:10:52.520
about 10%.

1820
01:10:52.520 --> 01:10:53.960
The receding crack epidemic,

1821
01:10:54.360 --> 01:10:55.300
about 6%.

1822
01:10:55.300 --> 01:10:56.640
And more police officers,

1823
01:10:57.120 --> 01:10:58.440
about 5.5%.

1824
01:10:58.440 --> 01:10:59.200
Together,

1825
01:10:59.520 --> 01:11:01.620
roughly 36 of the 43 points.

1826
01:11:02.140 --> 01:11:03.900
Six factors do not explain it.

1827
01:11:04.120 --> 01:11:05.580
Increased capital punishment,

1828
01:11:06.140 --> 01:11:07.620
about 1.5%.

1829
01:11:07.620 --> 01:11:09.200
Innovative policing strategies,

1830
01:11:09.600 --> 01:11:10.740
about 1%.

1831
01:11:10.740 --> 01:11:11.760
And the strong economy,

1832
01:11:11.980 --> 01:11:12.840
changing demographics,

1833
01:11:13.360 --> 01:11:14.380
gun control laws,

1834
01:11:14.520 --> 01:11:15.900
and concealed carry laws,

1835
01:11:16.100 --> 01:11:17.220
all essentially zero.

1836
01:11:17.720 --> 01:11:18.680
Now compare,

1837
01:11:18.880 --> 01:11:22.180
that list to what the 10 largest American newspapers actually

1838
01:11:22.180 --> 01:11:23.840
wrote about over the same decade.

1839
01:11:24.080 --> 01:11:26.640
Innovative policing was mentioned 52 times,

1840
01:11:26.860 --> 01:11:27.220
prisons,

1841
01:11:27.520 --> 01:11:28.480
47 times,

1842
01:11:28.780 --> 01:11:29.700
the strong economy,

1843
01:11:29.980 --> 01:11:30.860
28 times,

1844
01:11:31.060 --> 01:11:32.360
and legalized abortion,

1845
01:11:32.620 --> 01:11:34.700
which is Levitt's second largest factor,

1846
01:11:34.900 --> 01:11:36.260
was mentioned zero times,

1847
01:11:36.460 --> 01:11:37.140
not once.

1848
01:11:37.720 --> 01:11:40.720
Popular explanations get ranked by how satisfying they are.

1849
01:11:40.740 --> 01:11:42.640
Not by how much they explain.

1850
01:11:42.840 --> 01:11:43.700
That is the lesson.

1851
01:11:44.060 --> 01:11:46.900
And Levitt closes with a puzzle worth remembering,

1852
01:11:47.120 --> 01:11:50.700
run the same accounting on the period from 1973 to 1991,

1853
01:11:51.180 --> 01:11:52.940
and crime should have fallen then too.

1854
01:11:53.180 --> 01:11:53.940
It did not,

1855
01:11:54.080 --> 01:11:54.840
as he puts it,

1856
01:11:55.020 --> 01:11:58.160
the real puzzle is not why crime fell in the 1990s,

1857
01:11:58.300 --> 01:12:00.320
but why it did not start falling sooner.

1858
01:12:01.580 --> 01:12:04.680
Now let me tell you what the exams actually ask.

1859
01:12:05.480 --> 01:12:08.040
Professor Trounstein's stated goal is that you can analyze,

1860
01:12:08.400 --> 01:12:08.880
explain,

1861
01:12:08.880 --> 01:12:11.010
and apply your knowledge to new and different contexts,

1862
01:12:11.680 --> 01:12:15.140
so expect an unfamiliar claim and a request to evaluate it.

1863
01:12:15.440 --> 01:12:17.520
Five frames cover most of what you will see.

1864
01:12:18.000 --> 01:12:18.840
Frame 1.

1865
01:12:19.040 --> 01:12:20.460
Here is a claim from the news.

1866
01:12:20.900 --> 01:12:21.600
Evaluate it.

1867
01:12:21.800 --> 01:12:23.540
Run the four hurdles in order,

1868
01:12:23.680 --> 01:12:24.480
and say yes,

1869
01:12:24.840 --> 01:12:27.040
no or maybe to each one with a reason attached.

1870
01:12:27.260 --> 01:12:29.700
Name the specific confounder you are worried about,

1871
01:12:29.940 --> 01:12:31.240
not just other factors.

1872
01:12:31.240 --> 01:12:34.540
Then finish by saying what evidence would settle the question.

1873
01:12:34.740 --> 01:12:37.840
Usually that is an experiment that randomly assigned X,

1874
01:12:38.040 --> 01:12:41.300
and then you explain why that experiment is impossible or

1875
01:12:41.300 --> 01:12:42.000
unethical here,

1876
